## **SUMMARY: The Earliest Posits of the Son**\
\
From these four foundational texts \'97 given centuries before any council, any creed, any patristic speculation \'97 we have, by *plain reading* and *two-or-more witnesses*:\
\
| Text | What is revealed of the Son |\
|---|---|\
| **Gen 3:15** | A single Man, Seed of the woman, crusher of the serpent |\
| **Gen 22** | The Beloved Only-Begotten Son, offered by the Father, the Lamb God provides |\
| **Gen 49:10** | The Ruler from Judah, expectation of the nations |\
| **Num 24:17** | The Star and Man arising from Israel |\
\
**Notice carefully what these earliest prophecies do *not* say:** They do not call the Son "God." They do not say He pre-existed. They do not place Him at creation. They speak of Him as a **Man** \'97 beloved, only-begotten, born of a woman, of the tribe of Judah, rising from Israel.\
\
This is the **proper foundation**. Whatever further glories Scripture reveals about the Son must be built upon **this** foundation \'97 not in violation of the Father's exclusive deity declared in Isaiah 44-46, but in the manner Scripture itself unfolds.\
\
---\
\
Shall we now proceed to the next stage of revelation \'97 the **Psalms and Prophets**, where the Son is given titles of greater dignity (Son of God, Anointed, Lord at the right hand)? I would suggest beginning with **Psalm 2** and **Psalm 110 (LXX 109)**, which are the most cited Old Testament texts in the New Testament concerning the Son.\
\
\
Yes, let us examine Psalm 2 and Psalm 110 to see how the Son is further revealed as the Anointed and the Lord at the right hand.\
\
\
Excellent. These two Psalms are the **most quoted Old Testament texts in the New Testament** concerning the Son. They are the keystone of the apostolic preaching about who Jesus is and what office He holds in relation to the One God, the Father.\
