### Observation \'97 a critical LXX reading\
\
The LXX preserves a remarkable line absent from the standard Hebrew rendering of the Geneva: **\uc0\u7952 \u954  \u947 \u945 \u963 \u964 \u961 \u8056 \u962  \u960 \u961 \u8056  \u7953 \u969 \u963 \u966 \u972 \u961 \u959 \u965  \u7952 \u947 \u941 \u957 \u957 \u951 \u963 \u940  \u963 \u949 ** \'97 *"From the womb, before the morning star, I have begotten Thee."*\
\
This is the Father speaking again, as in Psalm 2:7. Here a **prior begetting** is in view \'97 **\uc0\u960 \u961 \u8056  \u7953 \u969 \u963 \u966 \u972 \u961 \u959 \u965 ** \'97 *"before the morning star"* \'97 that is, before the created luminaries of Genesis 1:14-16.\
\
This is the **second witness** (alongside Psalm 2:7) to the *begetting* of the Son \'97 but here the LXX places it **before** the cosmic order, while Psalm 2:7 places it on the day of resurrection. Scripture itself is presenting **two distinct begettings** \'97 or more carefully, a foreordained begetting from before time and its historical fulfillment in the resurrection. We will need to examine this carefully later, particularly with Proverbs 8 and Micah 5:2.\
\
For now, we simply note: **the Son is begotten of the Father** \'97 never the reverse, never the Father begotten of the Son, and never a "co-eternal" begetting where the Son has no origin in the Father. The Father is *Source*; the Son is *Begotten*.\
\
