## **A Question Now Properly Opened**\
\
Micah 5:2 spoke of the Son's **goings-forth** (plural) **from the beginning**. We have accounted for:\
- His begetting (Prov 8 / Ps 109:3 LXX).\
- His birth in Bethlehem (Mic 5:2 / Isa 7:14).\
\
But what of the **intermediate goings-forth** \'97 His appearances in the Old Testament between His begetting and His birth?\
\
Scripture testifies to a Person called **the Angel of the LORD** (**\uc0\u8001  \u7940 \u947 \u947 \u949 \u955 \u959 \u962  \u922 \u965 \u961 \u943 \u959 \u965 ** \'97 literally, *the Messenger of the LORD*) who appears repeatedly \'97 to Hagar, Abraham, Jacob, Moses, Gideon, Manoah \'97 and who *speaks as God*, is *worshipped*, yet is *sent from God*. Is this Person the pre-incarnate Son \'97 one of the **\u7956 \u958 \u959 \u948 \u959 \u953 ** of Micah 5:2?\
\
Shall we proceed there next \'97 examining the **Angel of the LORD** as a witness to the pre-incarnate goings-forth of the Son?\
\
\
Yes, let us examine the Angel of the LORD passages \'97 beginning with Hagar in Genesis 16 and Abraham in Genesis 22 \'97 to see whether this Messenger is indeed the pre-incarnate Son.\
\
\
Excellent. We now examine one of the most consequential threads in all of Scripture \'97 the appearances of **\uc0\u8001  \u7940 \u947 \u947 \u949 \u955 \u959 \u962  \u922 \u965 \u961 \u943 \u959 \u965 ** (*the Messenger of the LORD*). We must let the texts speak for themselves and establish, by two-or-more witnesses, whether this Messenger is:\
\
(a) merely a created angel acting as God's spokesman, or\
(b) the One God Himself appearing, or\
(c) a distinct Person who speaks *as* God yet is *sent by* God \'97 i.e., the pre-incarnate Son in one of His **\uc0\u7956 \u958 \u959 \u948 \u959 \u953 ** (Micah 5:2).\
\
Per our maxims, we will not impose the answer; we will let Scripture deliver it.\
\
