## **What We Can Legitimately Say About the Messenger Passages**\
\
Let us state plainly what the texts deliver:\
\
1. **The One God of the Shema** (Deut 6:4; Isa 44-46) \'97 the Father \'97 is invisible, unique, and stretches out the heavens alone. *This is foundation.*\
\
2. **The Messenger of the LORD** appears, speaks, and is treated as God \'97 yet is also sent by the LORD and bears the LORD's Name. The text creates a real and intentional distinction between **the One who sends** and **the One sent**, while the Sent One carries the full authority and Name of the Sender.\
\
3. **What this means precisely** \'97 whether the Messenger is the Son, or a manifestation of the LORD Himself in visible form, or a created spokesman bearing the LORD's authority \'97 **the OT text does not say in those words**, and the NT does not explicitly retroject the identification onto these specific passages.\
\
We may **note** the parallels (the Father's Name dwelling in Him \'97 Ex 23:21 / John 17:11-12; the One who is "with God" and acts as God's agent \'97 Prov 8 / John 1:1-3) \'97 but we must label these as **observations awaiting fuller establishment**, not as proven doctrine.\
