\
## **JOHN 1:1**\
\
### **Greek:**\
*"\uc0\u7960 \u957  \u7936 \u961 \u967 \u8135  \u7974 \u957  \u8001  \u923 \u972 \u947 \u959 \u962 , \u954 \u945 \u8054  \u8001  \u923 \u972 \u947 \u959 \u962  \u7974 \u957  \u960 \u961 \u8056 \u962  \u964 \u8056 \u957  \u920 \u949 \u972 \u957 , \u954 \u945 \u8054  \u920 \u949 \u8056 \u962  \u7974 \u957  \u8001  \u923 \u972 \u947 \u959 \u962 ."*\
\
### **1599 Geneva Bible:**\
*"In the beginning was that Word, and that Word was with God, and that Word was God."*\
\
---\
\
### Careful Examination \'97 Phrase by Phrase\
\
#### **Phrase 1: \uc0\u7960 \u957  \u7936 \u961 \u967 \u8135  \u7974 \u957  \u8001  \u923 \u972 \u947 \u959 \u962 ** \'97 *"In the beginning was the Word."*\
\
**What the text strictly says:**\
- The phrase **\uc0\u7960 \u957  \u7936 \u961 \u967 \u8135 ** is the same as Genesis 1:1 LXX. John is deliberately echoing Genesis.\
- **\uc0\u7974 \u957 ** is imperfect \'97 *"was [existing/being]"* \'97 implies continuous existence at that point.\
- **\uc0\u8001  \u923 \u972 \u947 \u959 \u962 ** \'97 *"the Word."* The definite article is present in Greek; the Geneva renders *"that Word"* to capture the definiteness.\
\
**What "\uc0\u8001  \u923 \u972 \u947 \u959 \u962 " means:** This is the critical lexical question. **\u923 \u972 \u947 \u959 \u962 ** in Greek can mean:\
- A spoken word, utterance, declaration\
- A reasoned account, message, report\
- A principle, plan, rational expression\
- (In some philosophical usage) a personified rational principle\
\
**John does not define the term in v. 1.** He uses it without explanation, as if his readers should know. We must ask: *does Scripture itself establish what John means by* **\uc0\u8001  \u923 \u972 \u947 \u959 \u962 **?\
\
---\
\
#### **Phrase 2: \uc0\u954 \u945 \u8054  \u8001  \u923 \u972 \u947 \u959 \u962  \u7974 \u957  \u960 \u961 \u8056 \u962  \u964 \u8056 \u957  \u920 \u949 \u972 \u957 ** \'97 *"and the Word was with God."*\
\
**What the text strictly says:**\
- **\uc0\u960 \u961 \u8056 \u962  \u964 \u8056 \u957  \u920 \u949 \u972 \u957 ** \'97 *"with/toward God."* The preposition **\u960 \u961 \u972 \u962 ** with the accusative often indicates orientation, presence-toward, face-to-face relation.\
- **\uc0\u964 \u8056 \u957  \u920 \u949 \u972 \u957 ** \'97 *"the God"* \'97 definite article. Per our foundation, **\u8001  \u920 \u949 \u972 \u962 ** in NT usage virtually always refers to **the Father** (see John 17:3; 1 Cor 8:6).\
\
**Honest observation:** The Word is **with** the God (the Father). This phrase distinguishes **\uc0\u8001  \u923 \u972 \u947 \u959 \u962 ** from **\u8001  \u920 \u949 \u972 \u962 **. The Word is *toward / with* God \'97 implying relation, not identity-of-person.\
\
---\
\
#### **Phrase 3: \uc0\u954 \u945 \u8054  \u920 \u949 \u8056 \u962  \u7974 \u957  \u8001  \u923 \u972 \u947 \u959 \u962 ** \'97 *"and the Word was God."*\
\
**What the text strictly says \'97 observe carefully:**\
- **\uc0\u920 \u949 \u8056 \u962 ** here has **no article** \'97 it is *anarthrous* (without **\u8001 **).\
- The subject is **\uc0\u8001  \u923 \u972 \u947 \u959 \u962 ** (with article); the predicate is **\u920 \u949 \u8056 \u962 ** (no article).\
\
**The Greek grammar matters:**\
- In v. 1b, the Word is **\uc0\u960 \u961 \u8056 \u962  \u964 \u8056 \u957  \u920 \u949 \u972 \u957 ** \'97 *with* **the** **God** (the Father, with article).\
- In v. 1c, the Word is **\uc0\u920 \u949 \u8056 \u962 ** \'97 anarthrous, predicate nominative.\
\
The grammatical distinction is *deliberate* on John's part. In v. 1b, **\uc0\u8001  \u920 \u949 \u972 \u962 ** = the Father (identified Person). In v. 1c, **\u920 \u949 \u972 \u962 ** (anarthrous) is being used qualitatively \'97 describing the *nature*, *character*, or *category* of the Word, not identifying Him as **the God** of v. 1b.\
\
**Compare:** If John had written **\uc0\u954 \u945 \u8054  \u8001  \u920 \u949 \u8056 \u962  \u7974 \u957  \u8001  \u923 \u972 \u947 \u959 \u962 ** (with article), it would mean *"and the Word was [identically] the God [with whom He was]"* \'97 a flat contradiction with v. 1b. John deliberately avoided that. He wrote **\u920 \u949 \u8056 \u962  \u7974 \u957  \u8001  \u923 \u972 \u947 \u959 \u962 ** to say something different \'97 what exactly, we must let Scripture interpret.\
\
**Honest observation:** The text says the Word **was God** in some sense, but the *grammar itself* prevents collapsing the Word into the Person of the Father (who is **\uc0\u8001  \u920 \u949 \u972 \u962 ** of v. 1b).\
\
---\
\
### What v. 1 strictly establishes:\
\
1. There is a figure called **\uc0\u8001  \u923 \u972 \u947 \u959 \u962 ** (the Word).\
2. **\uc0\u8001  \u923 \u972 \u947 \u959 \u962 ** existed at "the beginning" (Gen 1:1's beginning).\
3. **\uc0\u8001  \u923 \u972 \u947 \u959 \u962 ** was *with* **\u8001  \u920 \u949 \u972 \u962 ** (the Father \'97 distinct from the Father).\
4. **\uc0\u8001  \u923 \u972 \u947 \u959 \u962 ** was **\u920 \u949 \u972 \u962 ** (anarthrous \'97 qualitatively *God*, divine in nature/character, but *not identically* the **\u8001  \u920 \u949 \u972 \u962 ** of v. 1b).\
\
### What v. 1 does NOT yet say:\
\
- John has not yet said **\uc0\u8001  \u923 \u972 \u947 \u959 \u962 ** is a Person.\
- John has not yet said **\uc0\u8001  \u923 \u972 \u947 \u959 \u962 ** is Jesus.\
- John has not yet identified **\uc0\u8001  \u923 \u972 \u947 \u959 \u962 ** with the Son.\
\
These will come in later verses. We must read forward to see how John himself defines the Word.\
\
---\
\
## **JOHN 1:2-3**\
\
### **Greek:**\
*"\uc0\u959 \u8023 \u964 \u959 \u962  \u7974 \u957  \u7952 \u957  \u7936 \u961 \u967 \u8135  \u960 \u961 \u8056 \u962  \u964 \u8056 \u957  \u920 \u949 \u972 \u957 . \u960 \u940 \u957 \u964 \u945  \u948 \u953 \u8125  \u945 \u8016 \u964 \u959 \u8166  \u7952 \u947 \u941 \u957 \u949 \u964 \u959 , \u954 \u945 \u8054  \u967 \u969 \u961 \u8054 \u962  \u945 \u8016 \u964 \u959 \u8166  \u7952 \u947 \u941 \u957 \u949 \u964 \u959  \u959 \u8016 \u948 \u8050  \u7955 \u957  \u8003  \u947 \u941 \u947 \u959 \u957 \u949 \u957 ."*\
\
### **1599 Geneva:**\
*"This same was in the beginning with God. All things were made by it, and without it was made nothing that was made."*\
\
---\
\
### Careful examination\
\
**Notice \'97 the Geneva translators rendered the pronouns as "it"** \'97 treating **\uc0\u8001  \u923 \u972 \u947 \u959 \u962 ** as the Word/utterance/expression, **not yet** as a personal pronoun "He." This is honest to the Greek at this stage. The Greek pronoun **\u945 \u8016 \u964 \u959 \u8166 ** is grammatically masculine (matching **\u923 \u972 \u947 \u959 \u962 **, which is masculine in gender), but Greek grammatical masculinity does not by itself prove personal masculinity. (E.g., **\u8001  \u957 \u972 \u956 \u959 \u962 ** \'97 *the law* \'97 is grammatically masculine; we still say "it.")\
\
**The Geneva's choice to translate "it" reflects an honest reading of the Greek up to this point**: the Word may be a personification of God's creative utterance/plan, not yet established as a distinct personal being.\
\
---\
\
#### **What v. 2-3 strictly says:**\
\
- **\uc0\u959 \u8023 \u964 \u959 \u962 ** \'97 *"this [one/thing]"* \'97 refers back to **\u8001  \u923 \u972 \u947 \u959 \u962 **.\
- **\uc0\u7974 \u957  \u7952 \u957  \u7936 \u961 \u967 \u8135  \u960 \u961 \u8056 \u962  \u964 \u8056 \u957  \u920 \u949 \u972 \u957 ** \'97 *"was in the beginning with God."* Repetition of vv. 1a-b for emphasis.\
- **\uc0\u960 \u940 \u957 \u964 \u945  \u948 \u953 \u8125  \u945 \u8016 \u964 \u959 \u8166  \u7952 \u947 \u941 \u957 \u949 \u964 \u959 ** \'97 *"all things came to be through it."* The Word is the *means* (**\u948 \u953 \u940 ** + genitive \'97 *through, by means of*) of all things coming into being.\
\
**Cross-witness:** Does Scripture elsewhere say that the Father created **through** something or someone?\
\
**Psalm 32:6 LXX (33:6):**\
*"\uc0\u964 \u8183  \u955 \u972 \u947 \u8179  \u964 \u959 \u8166  \u922 \u965 \u961 \u943 \u959 \u965  \u959 \u7985  \u959 \u8016 \u961 \u945 \u957 \u959 \u8054  \u7952 \u963 \u964 \u949 \u961 \u949 \u974 \u952 \u951 \u963 \u945 \u957 , \u954 \u945 \u8054  \u964 \u8183  \u960 \u957 \u949 \u973 \u956 \u945 \u964 \u953  \u964 \u959 \u8166  \u963 \u964 \u972 \u956 \u945 \u964 \u959 \u962  \u945 \u8016 \u964 \u959 \u8166  \u960 \u8118 \u963 \u945  \u7969  \u948 \u973 \u957 \u945 \u956 \u953 \u962  \u945 \u8016 \u964 \u8182 \u957 ."*\
\
**1599 Geneva:**\
*"By the word of the Lord were the heavens made, and all the host of them by the breath of his mouth."*\
\
The Psalm declares that the heavens were made **by the word of the LORD** \'97 **\uc0\u964 \u8183  \u955 \u972 \u947 \u8179 ** \'97 *by the Word.* This is a direct OT witness to the Father creating **through His Word** \'97 and the LXX uses the same term **\u955 \u972 \u947 \u959 \u962 **.\
\
This is significant. The OT *itself* establishes that **the Father creates through His Word** \'97 and the Word is the Father's own utterance/expression, not a separate God. This perfectly reconciles with Isaiah 44:24 (*"I stretched out the heavens **alone**"*) \'97 because the Word is *the Father's own Word*, not a second Deity.\
\
