## **JOHN 1:9-11**\
\
### **Greek:**\
*"\uc0\u7982 \u957  \u964 \u8056  \u966 \u8182 \u962  \u964 \u8056  \u7936 \u955 \u951 \u952 \u953 \u957 \u972 \u957 , \u8003  \u966 \u969 \u964 \u943 \u950 \u949 \u953  \u960 \u940 \u957 \u964 \u945  \u7940 \u957 \u952 \u961 \u969 \u960 \u959 \u957 , \u7952 \u961 \u967 \u972 \u956 \u949 \u957 \u959 \u957  \u949 \u7984 \u962  \u964 \u8056 \u957  \u954 \u972 \u963 \u956 \u959 \u957 . \u7952 \u957  \u964 \u8183  \u954 \u972 \u963 \u956 \u8179  \u7974 \u957 , \u954 \u945 \u8054  \u8001  \u954 \u972 \u963 \u956 \u959 \u962  \u948 \u953 \u8125  \u945 \u8016 \u964 \u959 \u8166  \u7952 \u947 \u941 \u957 \u949 \u964 \u959 , \u954 \u945 \u8054  \u8001  \u954 \u972 \u963 \u956 \u959 \u962  \u945 \u8016 \u964 \u8056 \u957  \u959 \u8016 \u954  \u7956 \u947 \u957 \u969 . \u949 \u7984 \u962  \u964 \u8048  \u7988 \u948 \u953 \u945  \u7974 \u955 \u952 \u949 \u957 , \u954 \u945 \u8054  \u959 \u7985  \u7988 \u948 \u953 \u959 \u953  \u945 \u8016 \u964 \u8056 \u957  \u959 \u8016  \u960 \u945 \u961 \u941 \u955 \u945 \u946 \u959 \u957 ."*\
\
### **1599 Geneva:**\
*"This was that true light, which lighteth every man that cometh into the world. He was in the world, and the world was made by him: and the world knew him not. He came unto his own, and his own received him not."*\
\
---\
\
### Observations\
\
**(1) Important shift in the Geneva translation:** Up to v. 8, the Geneva used "it" for the Word. **At v. 10, the Geneva shifts to "him."** Why? Because the Light/Word is now described as coming **into the world** and **into His own** \'97 and being **received** or **not received** \'97 which are personal actions toward a personal subject. The Geneva translators are honest: they shifted to personal pronouns at the point where the Word's actions become personal.\
\
**(2)** **\uc0\u8001  \u954 \u972 \u963 \u956 \u959 \u962  \u948 \u953 \u8125  \u945 \u8016 \u964 \u959 \u8166  \u7952 \u947 \u941 \u957 \u949 \u964 \u959 ** \'97 *"the world was made through Him."* Repetition of v. 3's claim, now with personal pronoun.\
\
**(3)** **\uc0\u949 \u7984 \u962  \u964 \u8048  \u7988 \u948 \u953 \u945  \u7974 \u955 \u952 \u949 \u957 ** \'97 *"He came unto His own."* **\u964 \u8048  \u7988 \u948 \u953 \u945 ** \'97 *"His own things/people."* The Word has *His own* \'97 implying ownership, a prior relation to the world He made.\
\
**(4)** **\uc0\u959 \u7985  \u7988 \u948 \u953 \u959 \u953  \u945 \u8016 \u964 \u8056 \u957  \u959 \u8016  \u960 \u945 \u961 \u941 \u955 \u945 \u946 \u959 \u957 ** \'97 *"His own [people] did not receive Him."* This refers to Israel \'97 His covenant people. The Light came to Israel and was rejected.\
\
---\
\
### Honest assessment of vv. 9-11:\
\
These verses speak of the Word/Light **coming into the world** and **being rejected by His own**. The language is increasingly personal. *If* one reads this as the historical Jesus coming to Israel and being rejected, the actions are clearly personal. *If* one reads "He came into the world" as referring to a pre-incarnate coming, then a personal pre-existent Word is in view.\
\
The text moves seamlessly from creation-context (v. 10b: *"the world was made through Him"*) to incarnation-context (v. 11: *"His own did not receive Him"*) \'97 suggesting John sees these as actions of the *same* Subject. This is **a stronger push** toward personal pre-existence than vv. 1-5 alone provide.\
\
