### Verse 15 \'97 John the Baptist's Testimony\
\
**\uc0\u8001  \u8000 \u960 \u943 \u963 \u969  \u956 \u959 \u965  \u7952 \u961 \u967 \u972 \u956 \u949 \u957 \u959 \u962  \u7956 \u956 \u960 \u961 \u959 \u963 \u952 \u941 \u957  \u956 \u959 \u965  \u947 \u941 \u947 \u959 \u957 \u949 \u957 , \u8005 \u964 \u953  \u960 \u961 \u8182 \u964 \u972 \u962  \u956 \u959 \u965  \u7974 \u957 **\
\
*"He that comes after me has come to be before me, because He was first/prior to me."*\
\
This is a significant statement. John the Baptist was **born before** Jesus (six months earlier, per Luke 1:36). Yet John says Jesus **was prior** to him (**\uc0\u960 \u961 \u8182 \u964 \u972 \u962  \u956 \u959 \u965  \u7974 \u957 **). The natural reading: John is testifying to a *priority of being* of the One coming after him in time \'97 i.e., a pre-existence of some kind.\
\
**However**, the word **\uc0\u960 \u961 \u8182 \u964 \u959 \u962 ** can also mean *"of higher rank"* or *"chief"* \'97 and this is its meaning in some contexts. The Geneva captures this by translating: *"for he was better than I."* This rendering preserves the ambiguity \'97 John may be testifying to *priority of being* or to *priority of rank*.\
\
**Honest assessment:** v. 15 admits both readings.\
\
