### What Col 1:13-20 establishes:\
\
- The Son is the **Image** of the invisible Father.\
- The Son is **firstborn over all creation** (preeminent).\
- **All things were created in, through, and for Him.**\
- He is **before all things**.\
- **All things hold together in Him.**\
- **The Father's fullness dwells in Him** by the Father's pleasure.\
\
This passage, taken at face value, presents the Son as the agent of original creation \'97 which seems to stand in tension with Isaiah 44:24's *"I stretched out the heavens **alone**."*\
\
### Reconciliation honestly considered:\
\
The reconciliation must lie in this: **the Son acts as the Father's own agent**, so that what the Father does *through* the Son is still the Father's own act, done *alone* in the sense that no other Deity participates. The Son is not "another God"; He is the Father's own Image, through whom the Father works. The Father's **Word** (Ps 33:6) creates \'97 yet the Word is the Father's own, not a rival.\
\
This reading honors both Isa 44:24 and Col 1:16. The Father stretches out the heavens alone \'97 *through His own Image, His own Firstborn, His own Word.*\
