**(1)** Jesus' own dying cry from the cross is **a direct quotation of Psalm 21:1 LXX (22:1 MT).** This is the strongest possible witness: **Jesus Himself identifies His suffering with this Psalm.** He does not merely quote a phrase from Scripture; He recites the opening line, claiming the entire Psalm as His own.\
\
**(2)** **\uc0\u8009  \u920 \u949 \u972 \u962  \u956 \u959 \u965 ** \'97 *"My God."* The Christ on the cross addresses the Father as **My God.** This is consistent with John 20:17 \'97 *"I ascend to My Father and your Father, and My God and your God."* The Son has a God; that God is the Father. **Even at the climax of His suffering, the Son's monotheistic confession is preserved.**\
\
**(3)** **\uc0\u7952 \u947 \u954 \u945 \u964 \u941 \u955 \u953 \u960 \u941 \u962  \u956 \u949 ** \'97 *"You have forsaken Me."* The Father forsakes the Son in this moment. This is a real abandonment \'97 not a feigned one. The Son experiences true forsakenness while bearing sin.\
\
**(4)** Note the LXX reads **\uc0\u959 \u7985  \u955 \u972 \u947 \u959 \u953  \u964 \u8182 \u957  \u960 \u945 \u961 \u945 \u960 \u964 \u969 \u956 \u940 \u964 \u969 \u957  \u956 \u959 \u965 ** \'97 *"the words of my transgressions"* \'97 where the Hebrew has *"the words of my roaring."* The LXX makes the "transgressions" language explicit. Yet \'97 the Son had no transgressions of His own (Heb 4:15; 1 Pet 2:22). The "transgressions" must be the sins **He bore on behalf of others** (Isa 53:6, 11-12), which we shall examine shortly.\
