\
### Observations\
\
**(1)** **\uc0\u8036 \u961 \u965 \u958 \u945 \u957  \u967 \u949 \u8150 \u961 \u940 \u962  \u956 \u959 \u965  \u954 \u945 \u8054  \u960 \u972 \u948 \u945 \u962 ** \'97 *"they pierced/dug my hands and feet."* The verb **\u8000 \u961 \u973 \u963 \u963 \u969 ** means to dig, to pierce, to bore through. The LXX rendering is unambiguous: hands and feet are **pierced**.\
\
**(2) This is a remarkable witness.** The LXX was translated centuries before crucifixion was known as a Jewish or Christian theme. The reading "they pierced my hands and feet" is in the **pre-Christian Greek text**. The Masoretic Hebrew has a contested reading (\uc0\u1499 \u1464 \u1468 \u1488 \u1458 \u1512 \u1460 \u1497  \'97 *"like a lion"* vs. \u1499 \u1464 \u1468 \u1488 \u1458 \u1512 \u1493 \u1468  \'97 *"they pierced"*) that has been used by some to obscure the prophecy. The LXX preserves the unambiguous prophetic reading. **Per our maxim #1, we let God be true** \'97 and the God-breathed text Jesus and the Apostles used reads **\u8036 \u961 \u965 \u958 \u945 \u957 **.\
\
