# **SYNTHESIS \'97 What is Established by the Whole Counsel**\
\
By the most extensive and explicit OT-NT correspondence in Scripture, the following is established with overwhelming witness:\
\
### **The Suffering, Death, Burial, and Vindication of the Son**\
\
1. **The Son is the prophesied Servant of the LORD** \'97 identified explicitly by the Apostles (Acts 3:13, 26; 4:27, 30; 8:32-35), by Jesus Himself (Luke 22:37), and by the Father's voice at the baptism (using Isa 42:1 language).\
\
2. **He was forsaken by the Father on the cross**, bearing the cry of Psalm 22:1 (Matt 27:46; Mark 15:34).\
\
3. **He was pierced in His hands and feet**, with garments divided and lots cast (Ps 22:16, 18; all four Gospels; John 19:23-24 explicit).\
\
4. **He was silent before His accusers** as a lamb led to slaughter (Isa 53:7; Acts 8:32; Matt 27:12-14).\
\
5. **He bore the sins of many** \'97 making His soul a sin-offering (Isa 53:6, 10-12; 1 Pet 2:24; Rom 4:25; 8:32; Heb 9:28).\
\
6. **He was cut off from the land of the living** \'97 He really died (Isa 53:8).\
\
7. **He was buried with the rich** (Isa 53:9; Matt 27:57-60).\
\
8. **He was vindicated, seeing light and seed after His soul's travail** \'97 He rose from the dead (Isa 53:10-11; Acts 13:33 \'97 *"today have I begotten Thee"* fulfilled in resurrection).\
\
9. **He justifies many by His righteousness** (Isa 53:11; Rom 5:18-19).\
\
10. **The nations have heard and seen what was not previously declared** \'97 the Gospel goes to the Gentiles (Isa 52:15; Rom 15:21).\
\
---\
\
## **A Critical Theological Observation**\
\
Notice carefully what these prophecies establish \'97 and what they do **not** establish.\
\
### What they establish:\
\
- The Son's true humanity (a Man, with form, with thirst, with sorrow, with death).\
- The Son's true suffering (forsakenness, piercing, silence, death).\
- The Son's substitutionary work (He bore *our* sins; the LORD laid on Him the iniquity of us all).\
- The Son's vindication by the Father (resurrection, exaltation, justification of many).\
- **The Father's active role** as the One who *delivers Him up* (Isa 53:6, 10; Rom 8:32) and who *vindicates* Him.\
\
### What they do **not** establish:\
\
- They do not, by themselves, prove the Son's pre-existence or divinity. (Other passages, which we have already surveyed, do that \'97 Phil 2, John 17, John 8:58, Heb 1, etc.)\
- They speak of the Son as **the Servant**, **the Righteous One**, **the Man** \'97 and at no point in these passages is He called "God."\
\
### **What this confirms:**\
\
The pattern we have been building from the foundation upward holds firm:\
- **The Father is the One God** \'97 who delivers up His Servant for our sins.\
- **The Son is the Servant of the LORD** \'97 distinct from the Father, obedient unto death, vindicated by the Father.\
- **The Son's sufferings glorify the Father** \'97 and the Father's pleasure prospers in the Servant's hand (Isa 53:10).\
\
This is the **Servant Christology** of the earliest apostolic preaching (Acts 3:13, 26; 4:27, 30 \'97 all using **\uc0\u960 \u945 \u8150 \u962 **), and it is bound to the Father-Source / Son-Sent pattern we have established throughout.\
\
