### Observation\
\
**(1)** **\uc0\u7960 \u948 \u972 \u952 \u951  \u956 \u959 \u953  \u960 \u8118 \u963 \u945  \u7952 \u958 \u959 \u965 \u963 \u943 \u945 ** \'97 *"All authority **has been given** to Me."* Even after resurrection, the Son receives His authority **from** the Father. The passive verb is decisive.\
\
**(2)** The baptismal formula names **the Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit** \'97 though notice carefully that the **Name** (singular: **\uc0\u949 \u7984 \u962  \u964 \u8056  \u8004 \u957 \u959 \u956 \u945 **) is one Name, not three. This text deserves more careful examination later in our study, but at minimum it establishes that the Father, the Son, and the Spirit are jointly involved in the disciple-making mission \'97 though Jesus does *not* here teach a metaphysical doctrine of "three persons in one substance." He commands a baptism in a singular Name shared by the three.\
\
We should note, however, that the historical practice of the apostles in Acts is to baptize **"in the Name of Jesus Christ"** (Acts 2:38; 8:16; 10:48; 19:5). This warrants careful examination at the appropriate place \'97 perhaps when we treat the Apocalypse's view of "the Name."\
