**(1)** **\uc0\u946 \u945 \u963 \u953 \u955 \u949 \u8058 \u962  \u948 \u953 \u954 \u945 \u953 \u959 \u963 \u973 \u957 \u951 \u962  / \u946 \u945 \u963 \u953 \u955 \u949 \u8058 \u962  \u949 \u7984 \u961 \u942 \u957 \u951 \u962 ** \'97 *"King of Righteousness / King of Peace."* These titles, drawn from the Hebrew etymology, foreshadow the dual prophecies of Christ as the Righteous One who brings peace.\
\
**(2)** **\uc0\u7936 \u960 \u940 \u964 \u969 \u961 , \u7936 \u956 \u942 \u964 \u969 \u961 , \u7936 \u947 \u949 \u957 \u949 \u945 \u955 \u972 \u947 \u951 \u964 \u959 \u962 ** \'97 *"without father, without mother, without genealogy."* The author of Hebrews uses Melchizedek's *narrative silence* about his lineage as a typological device. Melchizedek appears and disappears without genealogical context \'97 making him a fitting *type* of an eternal Priest.\
\
**(3)** **\uc0\u7936 \u966 \u969 \u956 \u959 \u953 \u969 \u956 \u941 \u957 \u959 \u962  \u948 \u8050  \u964 \u8183  \u933 \u7985 \u8183  \u964 \u959 \u8166  \u920 \u949 \u959 \u8166 ** \'97 *"having been made like unto the Son of God."* This is critical. **Melchizedek is made like the Son** \'97 not the reverse. **The Son is the original; Melchizedek is the type.** This confirms the Son's pre-existence and priority \'97 He is the reality of which Melchizedek is the foreshadow.\
\
**(4)** **\uc0\u956 \u941 \u957 \u949 \u953  \u7985 \u949 \u961 \u949 \u8058 \u962  \u949 \u7984 \u962  \u964 \u8056  \u948 \u953 \u951 \u957 \u949 \u954 \u941 \u962 ** \'97 *"He continues a Priest perpetually."* Of Melchizedek as narrative figure (and ultimately of the Son whom he prefigures).\
