### Observation of considerable importance\
\
**Note the textual difference:**\
- **LXX:** **\uc0\u963 \u8182 \u956 \u945  \u948 \u8050  \u954 \u945 \u964 \u951 \u961 \u964 \u943 \u963 \u969  \u956 \u959 \u953 ** \'97 *"a body You prepared for Me."*\
- **Hebrew (followed by the 1599 Geneva):** *"mine ears hast thou prepared"* (or "digged/opened").\
\
**The author of Hebrews uses the LXX reading** \'97 **\uc0\u963 \u8182 \u956 \u945  \u948 \u8050  \u954 \u945 \u964 \u951 \u961 \u964 \u943 \u963 \u969  \u956 \u959 \u953 ** \'97 *"a body You prepared for Me."* Per our maxim #1 and our commitment to the LXX, this is the God-breathed reading the Apostle uses.\
\
This text is striking: it is presented as **the Son speaking** as He **enters the world** (**\uc0\u949 \u7984 \u963 \u949 \u961 \u967 \u972 \u956 \u949 \u957 \u959 \u962  \u949 \u7984 \u962  \u964 \u8056 \u957  \u954 \u972 \u963 \u956 \u959 \u957 **). The Son, on the eve of His incarnation, says to the Father: *"You did not want sacrifices and offerings \'97 but a body You prepared for Me. Behold, I come to do Your will, O God."*\
\
**The Son speaks of His own coming into the world as His act of obedience to the Father's will.** The Father prepared the body; the Son took it; the Son's purpose was to do the Father's will. This is the highest expression of the Father-Source / Son-Sent pattern, applied to the Incarnation itself.\
