## **CLAUSE-BY-CLAUSE EXAMINATION**\
\
### **Clause 1: \uc0\u949 \u7990 \u964 \u945  \u964 \u8056  \u964 \u941 \u955 \u959 \u962  \'97 *"Then comes the end"***\
\
**\uc0\u964 \u941 \u955 \u959 \u962 ** = *the end, the goal, the consummation.* Not merely a cessation, but the **completion** of God's purposes. There is a definite eschatological endpoint \'97 what Paul calls **"the end."**\
\
---\
\
### **Clause 2: \uc0\u8005 \u964 \u945 \u957  \u960 \u945 \u961 \u945 \u948 \u953 \u948 \u8183  \u964 \u8052 \u957  \u946 \u945 \u963 \u953 \u955 \u949 \u943 \u945 \u957  \u964 \u8183  \u920 \u949 \u8183  \u954 \u945 \u8054  \u928 \u945 \u964 \u961 \u943  \'97 *"when He delivers up the Kingdom to God, even the Father"***\
\
This is **the most consequential statement.** Let us examine it carefully.\
\
**\uc0\u960 \u945 \u961 \u945 \u948 \u953 \u948 \u8183 ** \'97 present subjunctive \'97 *"when He hands over, delivers up, surrenders."* The same verb used of Jesus being *"delivered up"* for our sins (Rom 4:25). Now the Son **delivers up** the Kingdom \'97 He hands it over.\
\
**\uc0\u964 \u8052 \u957  \u946 \u945 \u963 \u953 \u955 \u949 \u943 \u945 \u957 ** \'97 *"the Kingdom."* The very Kingdom that was given to Him by the Father (Dan 7:14; Matt 28:18) is now handed back.\
\
**\uc0\u964 \u8183  \u920 \u949 \u8183  \u954 \u945 \u8054  \u928 \u945 \u964 \u961 \u943 ** \'97 *"to the God and Father."* This is grammatically critical. **\u964 \u8183  \u920 \u949 \u8183  \u954 \u945 \u8054  \u928 \u945 \u964 \u961 \u943 ** uses **one article** (**\u964 \u8183 **) governing two designations connected by **\u954 \u945 \u943 ** \'97 meaning **the same Person** is identified as **both God and Father.** The dative singular construction makes it grammatically unmistakable: **God = the Father.** Not "God the Father" as one Person within a larger Godhead, but **"the God, even the Father"** \'97 the One God of the Shema, identified as the Father, just as we established at the foundation.\
\
The Geneva captures this exactly: *"to God, even the Father."*\
\
**This single phrase \'97 \uc0\u964 \u8183  \u920 \u949 \u8183  \u954 \u945 \u8054  \u928 \u945 \u964 \u961 \u943  \'97 is one of the most explicit statements in the NT that "God" is "the Father."** Per our foundation (Deut 6:4; Isa 44-46; John 17:3; 1 Cor 8:6), here it is again, plainly stated. **The Father is God.** The Son hands the Kingdom to **God, who is the Father.**\
