## **A Concluding Theological Observation**\
\
The doctrine of **eternal subordination of the Son to the Father** has been a contested point in church history. The post-Nicene party \'97 and especially the later Western tradition \'97 tended to insist that any "subordination" was *only* in the economy of salvation (His earthly mission), not in His eternal relation. But **Paul's text in 1 Cor 15:28 cannot bear that distinction.** Paul says explicitly: *"when all things shall be subdued unto Him, **then** shall the Son also himself be subject unto Him that did subdue all things under Him."*\
\
The subjection is **future** (**\uc0\u8017 \u960 \u959 \u964 \u945 \u947 \u942 \u963 \u949 \u964 \u945 \u953 **). It occurs **after** the present reign is complete. It is the **eternal posture** of the Son in the consummation. The Father is **eternally** the supreme One God; the Son is **eternally** the beloved Son who delights to be subject to His Father.\
\
Per our maxim #3, we do not need to soften this with post-Nicene metaphysical formulations. **Paul speaks plainly, and we let his words stand.**\
\
This does not diminish the Son. It is His glory and His joy. He has said throughout His ministry: *"I do always those things that please the Father"* (John 8:29). His eternal subjection to the Father is the eternal perfection of that filial obedience and love.\
