The greeting carefully **distinguishes the Father (the One who is, was, and is to come) from Jesus Christ**, with the seven Spirits between them. This is *not* a "trinitarian" formula in the post-Nicene sense \'97 the seven Spirits are not identical to "the Holy Spirit" in any creedal way, and the structure is not "three persons of one substance." But it is a **threefold greeting** that links the Father, the Spirit(s), and the Son in salutation \'97 preserving distinction throughout.\
