### Observations of supreme importance for our study\
\
**(1)** **\uc0\u7952 \u960 \u959 \u943 \u951 \u963 \u949 \u957  \u7969 \u956 \u8118 \u962  \u946 \u945 \u963 \u953 \u955 \u949 \u943 \u945 \u957 , \u7985 \u949 \u961 \u949 \u8150 \u962  \u964 \u8183  \u920 \u949 \u8183  \u954 \u945 \u8054  \u928 \u945 \u964 \u961 \u8054  \u945 \u8016 \u964 \u959 \u8166 ** \'97 *"He made us a Kingdom, priests **to the God and Father of Him**."*\
\
Notice carefully: **\uc0\u964 \u8183  \u920 \u949 \u8183  \u954 \u945 \u8054  \u928 \u945 \u964 \u961 \u8054  \u945 \u8016 \u964 \u959 \u8166 ** \'97 *"to His God and Father."* This is the same construction as **1 Cor 15:24** (*"to the God and Father"*) \'97 using one article governing two designations. The God is the Father.\
\
**And He is "His God and Father"** \'97 i.e., **the God of Jesus Christ.** This continues the consistent pattern: Jesus has a God; that God is the Father (John 20:17; Eph 1:17). Even in the Apocalypse, with all its exalted Christology, **the Father is the God of Jesus Christ.**\
\
**(2)** **\uc0\u945 \u8016 \u964 \u8183  \u7969  \u948 \u972 \u958 \u945  \u954 \u945 \u8054  \u964 \u8056  \u954 \u961 \u940 \u964 \u959 \u962 ** \'97 *"to Him be glory and dominion."* Doxology is rendered to Jesus. This is consistent with Phil 2:11 \'97 confession of Jesus as Lord, *to the glory of God the Father.* The doxology to the Son flows to the Father's glory.\
\
