## **QUESTION 2 \'97 How does the Father's Spirit-Nature undergird His invisibility yet His presence in all things?**\
\
---\
\
### **The Theological Resolution**\
\
The Father's Spirit-Nature is the metaphysical key that resolves what otherwise seems paradoxical:\
\
1. **He is invisible** (no man has seen Him \'97 John 1:18; 1 Tim 6:16) **because Spirit is not visible to physical sight.** Physical eyes perceive physical light; Spirit is of a different order of being.\
\
2. **He is omnipresent** (fills heaven and earth \'97 Jer 23:24) **because Spirit is not confined to a location.** Bodies occupy space; Spirit pervades it.\
\
3. **He is uncontainable** (the heavens cannot contain Him \'97 1 Kgs 8:27) **because Spirit cannot be enclosed by created boundaries.** Spatial structures cannot bound what is essentially non-spatial.\
\
4. **He can dwell** in His temple (1 Kgs 8:10-11), among His people (Ex 25:8), and ultimately in the redeemed (1 Cor 3:16-17; Eph 2:21-22) \'97 **because Spirit can pervade and indwell what physical bodies cannot.**\
\
5. **He has no body parts** in the literal sense \'97 when Scripture speaks of His "hand," "arm," "face," "eyes," "feet" \'97 these are **anthropomorphic accommodations** to creatures who think in physical terms. **The Father, as Spirit, does not have a physical body**, and the anthropomorphisms are metaphors of His action, presence, and disposition toward us.\
\
