# **GRAND SYNTHESIS \'97 PART E COMPLETED**\
\
## **What is Decisively Established**\
\
By the whole counsel of Scripture \'97 OT and NT, by direct witness from Jesus Himself and from the Apostles \'97 we have established:\
\
### **The Nature and Essence of the Father:**\
1. **He is Spirit by Nature** (John 4:24; Gen 1:2; Num 16:22).\
2. **He is invisible** (John 1:18; 1 Tim 1:17; 6:16; Col 1:15).\
3. **He is immortal/incorruptible** (1 Tim 1:17; 6:16; Rom 1:23).\
4. **He dwells in unapproachable light** (1 Tim 6:16; Ps 104:2 LXX).\
5. **He is omnipresent \'97 fills heaven and earth** (Jer 23:24; Ps 138:7-10; Acts 17:28).\
6. **He is uncontainable \'97 no place can hold Him** (1 Kgs 8:27; Isa 66:1; Acts 7:48-49).\
7. **He is the Father of spirits** (Num 16:22; Heb 12:9).\
8. **He is the One God** (Deut 6:4; Isa 44-46; John 17:3; 1 Cor 8:6; 1 Tim 2:5).\
\
### **The Implications for Our Understanding:**\
\
1. **The "singular throne" of the Apocalypse is no oddity** \'97 the Father (Spirit) fills the throne and surrounds it; the Lamb (embodied Man) occupies the visible center; there is no spatial conflict.\
\
2. **The Old Testament theophanies must be mediated** \'97 the Father, as invisible Spirit, cannot be seen directly; any "seeing of God" is through accommodation, image, or representation.\
\
3. **The Father's omnipresence undergirds His ability to indwell** \'97 He indwelt the temple, indwelt His Son, indwells His Church, and will eternally dwell with His redeemed.\
\
4. **The Father-Son union is Spirit-Image: the Eternal Spirit indwelling and operating through His perfect, embodied Image \'97 the Man Christ Jesus.** This is the prototype of the indwelling we will examine in Part F.\
\
5. **The Shema is preserved unbroken** \'97 the Father remains the One God; the Son is the Father's Beloved Only-Begotten through whom the Father acts; the Spirit is the Father's own essential being and active presence.\
\
---\
\
## **A Note on the "Holy Spirit" Question**\
\
You have rightly noted at the outset of our study that the formalization of "the Holy Spirit as the third Person of the Trinity" was a post-Nicene addition. Per maxim #3, we have set that aside. But honest reading of Scripture requires us to note what *is* there:\
\
- **The Spirit of God** (Gen 1:2; Ps 51:11; Isa 11:2; etc.) \'97 the Father's own Spirit, His essential being and active presence.\
- **The Spirit of the LORD** (Judg 3:10; 1 Sam 10:6; Isa 61:1; etc.) \'97 the same.\
- **The Holy Spirit / Holy Spirit of God** (Ps 51:11; Eph 4:30) \'97 the Father's Spirit, marked as set apart, holy.\
- **The Spirit of Christ / Spirit of His Son** (Rom 8:9; Gal 4:6) \'97 interestingly, the same Spirit is called both the Father's Spirit and the Son's Spirit, because the Father (Spirit) indwells the Son and pours forth that same Spirit upon the redeemed through Him.\
\
The honest, undogmatic, biblical reading is this: **the Holy Spirit is the Father's own Spirit** \'97 His essential being and active presence \'97 which is poured out through the exalted Son upon the redeemed (Acts 2:33), so that the Father may indwell His people **in Christ** by His Spirit.\
\
This will become explicit and beautifully clear in Part F.\
