### **Clause 1: \uc0\u964 \u8135  \u948 \u949 \u958 \u953 \u8119  \u959 \u8022 \u957  \u964 \u959 \u8166  \u920 \u949 \u959 \u8166  \u8017 \u968 \u969 \u952 \u949 \u8054 \u962 **\
\
*"Therefore, having been exalted by/to the right hand of God..."*\
\
**Critical grammatical note:** The phrase **\uc0\u964 \u8135  \u948 \u949 \u958 \u953 \u8119  \u964 \u959 \u8166  \u920 \u949 \u959 \u8166 ** is in the **dative case**. The dative can be read in two ways here:\
\
- **Instrumental dative:** *"by the right hand of God"* \'97 i.e., **the Father's right hand (His power/agency) raised the Son up.**\
- **Dative of location:** *"to the right hand of God"* \'97 i.e., **the Son was raised up to the place at the Father's right hand.**\
\
**Both readings are grammatically valid.** The Geneva translates: *"by the right hand of God hath been exalted."* This honestly captures the primary sense \'97 **the Father's agency exalted the Son** \'97 while also implying the location (the place to which He was exalted).\
\
**What is decisively established by both readings:**\
- **The Son did not exalt Himself.** The verb **\uc0\u8017 \u968 \u969 \u952 \u949 \u943 \u962 ** is a passive participle \'97 *"having been exalted."* He is the recipient of the action.\
- **The Father is the agent** \'97 the One who exalted Him.\
- **The Son's present location is at the Father's right hand** \'97 consistent with Psalm 110:1, which Peter explicitly cites in v. 34.\
\
This perfectly preserves our foundation: **the Father is the One who acts; the Son is the One who receives.** Even at His exaltation, the Son does not lift Himself \'97 the Father lifts Him.\
