\
## **JOHN 17:21 \'97 THE CENTRAL PETITION**\
\
### **Greek:**\
*"\uc0\u7989 \u957 \u945  \u960 \u940 \u957 \u964 \u949 \u962  \u7955 \u957  \u8038 \u963 \u953 \u957 , \u954 \u945 \u952 \u8060 \u962  \u963 \u973 , \u928 \u940 \u964 \u949 \u961 , \u7952 \u957  \u7952 \u956 \u959 \u8054  \u954 \u7936 \u947 \u8060  \u7952 \u957  \u963 \u959 \u943 , \u7989 \u957 \u945  \u954 \u945 \u8054  \u945 \u8016 \u964 \u959 \u8054  \u7952 \u957  \u7969 \u956 \u8150 \u957  \u8038 \u963 \u953 \u957 , \u7989 \u957 \u945  \u8001  \u954 \u972 \u963 \u956 \u959 \u962  \u960 \u953 \u963 \u964 \u949 \u973 \u8131  \u8005 \u964 \u953  \u963 \u973  \u956 \u949  \u7936 \u960 \u941 \u963 \u964 \u949 \u953 \u955 \u945 \u962 ."*\
\
### **1599 Geneva:**\
*"That they all may be one, as thou, O Father, art in me, and I in thee, even that they may be also one in us, that the world may believe, that thou hast sent me."*\
\
---\
\
### Let us examine this verse with the care it demands.\
\
This is **the central petition of the entire prayer**, and it contains four critical clauses:\
\
---\
\
#### **Clause 1: \uc0\u7989 \u957 \u945  \u960 \u940 \u957 \u964 \u949 \u962  \u7955 \u957  \u8038 \u963 \u953 \u957  \'97 *"that they all may be one"***\
\
**\uc0\u7955 \u957  \u8038 \u963 \u953 \u957 ** \'97 *"may be one."* The verb is **subjunctive** (purpose), and the predicate **\u7957 \u957 ** is neuter \'97 *"one [thing]."* Not numerically one (as in one person), but **unified in being / one in essential reality.**\
\
**The petition:** That all believers from Pentecost forward may be **one**. This is not asking for organizational unity, denominational uniformity, or political consolidation. This is a far deeper unity \'97 defined by the next clause.\
\
---\
\
#### **Clause 2: \uc0\u954 \u945 \u952 \u8060 \u962  \u963 \u973 , \u928 \u940 \u964 \u949 \u961 , \u7952 \u957  \u7952 \u956 \u959 \u8054  \u954 \u7936 \u947 \u8060  \u7952 \u957  \u963 \u959 \u943  \'97 *"even as Thou, Father, art in Me, and I in Thee"***\
\
**The model.** The oneness Jesus prays for is patterned after **the mutual indwelling of the Father and the Son.**\
\
**\uc0\u954 \u945 \u952 \u974 \u962 ** \'97 *"even as / according as / in the same manner as."* The believers' oneness is to be **of the same character** as the Father-Son oneness \'97 though, as we shall see, not identical in essence.\
\
**\uc0\u963 \u973 , \u928 \u940 \u964 \u949 \u961 , \u7952 \u957  \u7952 \u956 \u959 \u8054 ** \'97 *"Thou, Father, in Me."*\
**\uc0\u954 \u7936 \u947 \u8060  \u7952 \u957  \u963 \u959 \u943 ** \'97 *"and I in Thee."*\
\
**Mutual indwelling.** The Father is in the Son; the Son is in the Father. This is the eternal reality we established in Part E \'97 the Father (Spirit) indwells the Son (Image), and the Son is in the Father (in the Father's Spirit-presence).\
\
**The prototype is established.** The Father-Son union is the model for the believers' union.\
\
---\
\
#### **Clause 3: \uc0\u7989 \u957 \u945  \u954 \u945 \u8054  \u945 \u8016 \u964 \u959 \u8054  \u7952 \u957  \u7969 \u956 \u8150 \u957  \u8038 \u963 \u953 \u957  \'97 *"that they also may be in Us"***\
\
**Here is the breathtaking petition.** The Greek is: **\uc0\u954 \u945 \u8054  \u945 \u8016 \u964 \u959 \u8054 ** \'97 *"they also"* \'97 **\u7952 \u957  \u7969 \u956 \u8150 \u957 ** \'97 *"in Us"* \'97 **\u8038 \u963 \u953 \u957 ** \'97 *"may be."*\
\
**"In Us."** Believers are to be **in the Father and in the Son** \'97 drawn into the Father-Son mutual indwelling.\
\
This is staggering, and we must handle it with great care. Let me draw it out precisely:\
\
**The pattern of John 14:20 was:**\
- I am in the Father.\
- You are in Me.\
- I am in you.\
\
**The pattern of John 17:21 is:**\
- The Father is in Me; I am in the Father (mutual indwelling).\
- That **they may be in Us** \'97 drawn into this mutual indwelling.\
\
**The believer is not directly "in the Father" apart from the Son.** Note carefully: **\uc0\u7952 \u957  \u7969 \u956 \u8150 \u957 ** \'97 *"in Us."* The "Us" is the Father and the Son together. The believer is brought into the joint reality of the Father-Son union \'97 **through being in the Son who is in the Father.**\
\
This perfectly preserves what we established in our earlier examination of John 14:20:\
- **The believer's relation to the Father is mediated through the Son.**\
- The believer is *in the Son*; the Son is *in the Father*; therefore the believer is brought *into the Father-Son union*.\
- The Son remains the sole Mediator (1 Tim 2:5).\
\
---\
\
#### **Clause 4: \uc0\u7989 \u957 \u945  \u8001  \u954 \u972 \u963 \u956 \u959 \u962  \u960 \u953 \u963 \u964 \u949 \u973 \u8131  \u8005 \u964 \u953  \u963 \u973  \u956 \u949  \u7936 \u960 \u941 \u963 \u964 \u949 \u953 \u955 \u945 \u962  \'97 *"that the world may believe that Thou hast sent Me"***\
\
**The purpose of the oneness is missional.** The world's faith in the Father's sending of the Son depends on **the visible oneness of His people.** When the world sees believers genuinely united in the Father-Son reality, the world is given evidence that Jesus was indeed sent from God.\
\
This is a profound truth: **our oneness is the Father's evidence to the world.** The disunity of professing Christians has been a great hindrance to the Gospel's progress.\
\
