\
### **Clause 3: \uc0\u950 \u8182  \u948 \u8050  \u959 \u8016 \u954 \u941 \u964 \u953  \u7952 \u947 \u974 **\
\
*"I live; yet no longer I."*\
\
**This is the death of the self.** Paul lives \'97 but the "I" that lives is no longer the old Paul. The old man, the Adam-self, is gone. The Pharisee Saul of Tarsus has been put to death.\
\
The phrasing is paradoxical: **"I live, yet not I."** Paul does not deny his ongoing existence; he denies that the ongoing existence is the old self. The man who lives now is not who he was.\
\
**Cross-witness \'97 2 Corinthians 5:17 (Greek):**\
*"\uc0\u8037 \u963 \u964 \u949  \u949 \u7988  \u964 \u953 \u962  \u7952 \u957  \u935 \u961 \u953 \u963 \u964 \u8183 , \u954 \u945 \u953 \u957 \u8052  \u954 \u964 \u943 \u963 \u953 \u962 \'b7 \u964 \u8048  \u7936 \u961 \u967 \u945 \u8150 \u945  \u960 \u945 \u961 \u8134 \u955 \u952 \u949 \u957 , \u7984 \u948 \u959 \u8058  \u947 \u941 \u947 \u959 \u957 \u949 \u957  \u954 \u945 \u953 \u957 \u940 ."*\
\
**1599 Geneva:** *"Therefore if any man be in Christ, let him be a new creature. Old things are passed away: behold, all things are become new."*\
\
The believer in Christ is **a new creation.** The old has passed away. **This is established by two witnesses (Gal 2:20 and 2 Cor 5:17).**\
\
---\
\
### **Clause 4: \uc0\u950 \u8135  \u948 \u8050  \u7952 \u957  \u7952 \u956 \u959 \u8054  \u935 \u961 \u953 \u963 \u964 \u972 \u962 **\
\
*"but Christ lives in me."*\
\
**This is the indwelling we examined in Part F**, now stated in Paul's own personal experience.\
\
**(1)** **\uc0\u950 \u8135 ** \'97 present active \'97 *"He lives, continually lives."* Christ's living in Paul is **ongoing**, not a past event.\
\
**(2)** **\uc0\u7952 \u957  \u7952 \u956 \u959 \u943 ** \'97 *"in me."* The same preposition Jesus used in John 14:20 \'97 *"and I in you."* The indwelling promised by Jesus is the lived reality of the apostle.\
\
**(3) The chain:**\
- The believer **died with Christ** (Clause 2).\
- The believer's old self is **no longer living** (Clause 3).\
- **Christ now lives in the believer** (Clause 4).\
\
**The death of the old self made room for Christ's indwelling life.** This is the spiritual mechanism: the old must die before the new can live. The believer's ongoing existence is now the vehicle of Christ's life.\
\
---\
\
### **Clause 5: \uc0\u8003  \u948 \u8050  \u957 \u8166 \u957  \u950 \u8182  \u7952 \u957  \u963 \u945 \u961 \u954 \u943 , \u7952 \u957  \u960 \u943 \u963 \u964 \u949 \u953  \u950 \u8182  \u964 \u8135  \u964 \u959 \u8166  \u933 \u7985 \u959 \u8166  \u964 \u959 \u8166  \u920 \u949 \u959 \u8166 **\
\
*"And the life which I now live in the flesh, I live by faith of the Son of God."*\
\
**(1)** **\uc0\u957 \u8166 \u957  \u950 \u8182  \u7952 \u957  \u963 \u945 \u961 \u954 \u943 ** \'97 *"I now live in the flesh."* Paul acknowledges that he still lives in his physical body. The death of the old self does not mean physical death; the body remains. But the principle of life in the body has changed.\
\
**(2)** **\uc0\u7952 \u957  \u960 \u943 \u963 \u964 \u949 \u953  \u950 \u8182  \u964 \u8135  \u964 \u959 \u8166  \u933 \u7985 \u959 \u8166  \u964 \u959 \u8166  \u920 \u949 \u959 \u8166 ** \'97 *"I live in faith of the Son of God."*\
\
The genitive **\uc0\u964 \u959 \u8166  \u933 \u7985 \u959 \u8166  \u964 \u959 \u8166  \u920 \u949 \u959 \u8166 ** can be read either as:\
- **Objective genitive:** *"faith in the Son of God"* (the Son is the object of the believer's faith).\
- **Subjective genitive:** *"the faith of the Son of God"* (the Son's own faithfulness as the basis of our life).\
\
**Both readings carry profound truth, and both are likely intended.** The believer lives by faith in the Son, *and* the believer lives by the Son's own faithful self-giving. The Geneva preserves the ambiguity: *"by the faith in the Son of God."*\
\
---\
\
### **Clause 6: \uc0\u964 \u959 \u8166  \u7936 \u947 \u945 \u960 \u942 \u963 \u945 \u957 \u964 \u972 \u962  \u956 \u949  \u954 \u945 \u8054  \u960 \u945 \u961 \u945 \u948 \u972 \u957 \u964 \u959 \u962  \u7953 \u945 \u965 \u964 \u8056 \u957  \u8017 \u960 \u8050 \u961  \u7952 \u956 \u959 \u8166 **\
\
*"who loved me, and gave Himself for me."*\
\
**(1)** **\uc0\u964 \u959 \u8166  \u7936 \u947 \u945 \u960 \u942 \u963 \u945 \u957 \u964 \u972 \u962  \u956 \u949 ** \'97 *"the One who loved me."* Aorist participle \'97 a definite past act of love. The Son **loved** Paul (and every believer) with a specific, personal love.\
\
**(2)** **\uc0\u960 \u945 \u961 \u945 \u948 \u972 \u957 \u964 \u959 \u962  \u7953 \u945 \u965 \u964 \u8056 \u957  \u8017 \u960 \u8050 \u961  \u7952 \u956 \u959 \u8166 ** \'97 *"and gave Himself for me."* Aorist participle \'97 again a definite past act. The Son **gave Himself** (His own person, His own life) **for me** (on my behalf, in my place).\
\
**The personal pronouns are striking.** Paul does not say *"who loved us"* or *"who gave Himself for us"* \'97 but **"who loved ME and gave Himself for ME."** Each believer can say this personally. **The Son's love and self-giving is for me, individually, by name.**\
\
