\'85If we be children, we are also heirs, even the heirs of God, and heirs annexed with Christ: if so be that we suffer with him, that we may also be glorified with him.\
  \
What suffering(s) with Christ (in a practical sense) are endured by modern day believers?\
\
\
Excellent question \'97 and an essential one. This is **Romans 8:17**, and it is one of the most consequential conditional statements in the entire NT. Paul attaches a real condition to the believer's co-glorification: **"if so be that we suffer with Him."** We dare not pass over this casually.\
\
Let me address this carefully per our maxims:\
\
1. First, examine what the Greek of Romans 8:17 actually establishes (and what it does not).\
2. Survey the whole NT counsel on what "suffering with Christ" means.\
3. Bring this honestly into the present age for the modern believer.\
4. Be unflinching where Scripture is unflinching, and silent where Scripture is silent.\
\
---\
\
# **ROMANS 8:17 \'97 THE FOUNDATIONAL TEXT**\
\
### **Greek:**\
*"\uc0\u949 \u7984  \u948 \u8050  \u964 \u941 \u954 \u957 \u945 , \u954 \u945 \u8054  \u954 \u955 \u951 \u961 \u959 \u957 \u972 \u956 \u959 \u953 \'b7 \u954 \u955 \u951 \u961 \u959 \u957 \u972 \u956 \u959 \u953  \u956 \u8050 \u957  \u920 \u949 \u959 \u8166 , \u963 \u965 \u947 \u954 \u955 \u951 \u961 \u959 \u957 \u972 \u956 \u959 \u953  \u948 \u8050  \u935 \u961 \u953 \u963 \u964 \u959 \u8166 , \u949 \u7988 \u960 \u949 \u961  \u963 \u965 \u956 \u960 \u940 \u963 \u967 \u959 \u956 \u949 \u957  \u7989 \u957 \u945  \u954 \u945 \u8054  \u963 \u965 \u957 \u948 \u959 \u958 \u945 \u963 \u952 \u8182 \u956 \u949 \u957 ."*\
\
### **1599 Geneva:**\
*"If we be children, we are also heirs, even the heirs of God, and heirs annexed with Christ: if so be that we suffer with him, that we may also be glorified with him."*\
\
---\
\
### Careful examination of the conditional\
\
**\uc0\u949 \u7988 \u960 \u949 \u961  \u963 \u965 \u956 \u960 \u940 \u963 \u967 \u959 \u956 \u949 \u957 ** \'97 *"if indeed / since indeed we suffer together."*\
\
**\uc0\u949 \u7988 \u960 \u949 \u961 ** is a strengthened form of **\u949 \u7984 ** (if). It can be translated *"if indeed"* or *"inasmuch as"* \'97 sometimes carrying the sense of *"seeing that"* (assuming the condition is in fact met). It is **not** a doubtful "if" \'97 it is more like *"if, as is the case"* or *"given that."*\
\
**\uc0\u963 \u965 \u956 \u960 \u940 \u963 \u967 \u959 \u956 \u949 \u957 ** \'97 present active indicative \'97 *"we are suffering together [with Christ]."* The present tense indicates **an ongoing reality**, not a future possibility. Paul presents co-suffering as the present experience of the believer.\
\
**\uc0\u7989 \u957 \u945  \u954 \u945 \u8054  \u963 \u965 \u957 \u948 \u959 \u958 \u945 \u963 \u952 \u8182 \u956 \u949 \u957 ** \'97 *"in order that we may also be glorified together [with Him]."* Aorist passive subjunctive \'97 the purpose / result clause. **The co-suffering is the path to the co-glorification.**\
\
---\
\
### What this verse establishes\
\
**(1) Suffering with Christ is normative for the believer.** Paul does not say *"some believers suffer with Him."* He presents co-suffering as the present experience of those who are joint-heirs. The \uc0\u949 \u7988 \u960 \u949 \u961  does not introduce a possibility \'97 it confirms an actual condition that characterizes the believer's life.\
\
**(2) Co-suffering is the path to co-glorification.** The Father's design is that the path to glory runs through fellowship with the Son's sufferings. **There is no co-glorification without co-suffering.** This is not punishment for being a child of God; it is the **pattern of the Beloved Son**, into whose image we are being conformed (Rom 8:29).\
\
**(3) This is corporate to all believers, not an elite calling.** Paul includes himself and his Roman readers \'97 ordinary believers \'97 in the *"we"* who suffer together with Christ.\
\
---\
\
# **WHAT DOES "SUFFERING WITH CHRIST" MEAN?**\
\
Per our maxims, we must let Scripture itself define this \'97 not project our own assumptions. The NT identifies several distinct yet overlapping categories of suffering that constitute the believer's fellowship with Christ's sufferings.\
\
Let us survey them carefully.\
\
---\
\
## **CATEGORY 1 \'97 SUFFERING FOR RIGHTEOUSNESS' SAKE**\
\
This is the most explicit form of suffering with Christ: enduring opposition, hostility, slander, persecution, or harm specifically *because of one's union with Him and obedience to His word.*\
\
