# **ROMANS 6:1-2 \'97 THE QUESTION AND THE PIVOT**\
\
### **Greek:**\
*"\uc0\u932 \u943  \u959 \u8022 \u957  \u7952 \u961 \u959 \u8166 \u956 \u949 \u957 ; \u7952 \u960 \u953 \u956 \u941 \u957 \u969 \u956 \u949 \u957  \u964 \u8135  \u7937 \u956 \u945 \u961 \u964 \u943 \u8115 , \u7989 \u957 \u945  \u7969  \u967 \u940 \u961 \u953 \u962  \u960 \u955 \u949 \u959 \u957 \u940 \u963 \u8131 ; \u956 \u8052  \u947 \u941 \u957 \u959 \u953 \u964 \u959 \'b7 \u959 \u7989 \u964 \u953 \u957 \u949 \u962  \u7936 \u960 \u949 \u952 \u940 \u957 \u959 \u956 \u949 \u957  \u964 \u8135  \u7937 \u956 \u945 \u961 \u964 \u943 \u8115 , \u960 \u8182 \u962  \u7956 \u964 \u953  \u950 \u942 \u963 \u959 \u956 \u949 \u957  \u7952 \u957  \u945 \u8016 \u964 \u8135 ;"*\
\
### **1599 Geneva:**\
*"What shall we say then? Shall we continue still in sin, that grace may abound? God forbid: how shall we, that are dead to sin, live yet therein?"*\
\
---\
\
### Observations of foundational importance\
\
**(1)** Paul's response to the question *"shall we continue in sin?"* is **\uc0\u956 \u8052  \u947 \u941 \u957 \u959 \u953 \u964 \u959 ** \'97 *"God forbid"* / *"may it never be"* \'97 the strongest negation in Greek. The very thought is unthinkable.\
\
**(2)** But notice carefully **why** Paul says it is unthinkable. He does not say *"Because sin is bad."* He does not say *"Because God's law forbids it."* He says: **\uc0\u959 \u7989 \u964 \u953 \u957 \u949 \u962  \u7936 \u960 \u949 \u952 \u940 \u957 \u959 \u956 \u949 \u957  \u964 \u8135  \u7937 \u956 \u945 \u961 \u964 \u943 \u8115 , \u960 \u8182 \u962  \u7956 \u964 \u953  \u950 \u942 \u963 \u959 \u956 \u949 \u957  \u7952 \u957  \u945 \u8016 \u964 \u8135 ;** \'97 *"We who died to sin, how shall we still live in it?"*\
\
**This is the indicative.** Before Paul will give a single command in this chapter, he establishes the **fact** of what has happened to the believer: **we died to sin.** The aorist tense **\uc0\u7936 \u960 \u949 \u952 \u940 \u957 \u959 \u956 \u949 \u957 ** points to a definite past event \'97 **the believer's death to sin at the cross of Christ.**\
\
The argument is **ontological, not merely behavioral.** Paul does not argue that the believer *should not* continue in sin because it would be wrong. He argues that the believer *cannot consistently* continue in sin because **he has died to it.** Continuing in sin is **a contradiction of the new reality of his being.**\
\
This is the indicative-to-imperative pattern in its purest form: the moral question is answered by a declaration of fact.\
\
