# **THE OPENING FRAME \'97 PHILIPPIANS 2:5**\
\
Before we examine v. 6, we must note what Paul establishes as the *purpose* of citing this passage.\
\
### **Greek:**\
*"\uc0\u932 \u959 \u8166 \u964 \u959  \u966 \u961 \u959 \u957 \u949 \u8150 \u964 \u949  \u7952 \u957  \u8017 \u956 \u8150 \u957  \u8003  \u954 \u945 \u8054  \u7952 \u957  \u935 \u961 \u953 \u963 \u964 \u8183  \u7992 \u951 \u963 \u959 \u8166 ,"*\
\
### **1599 Geneva:**\
*"Let the same mind be in you that was even in Christ Jesus,"*\
\
---\
\
### Observations of foundational importance\
\
**(1) The context is ethical, not primarily metaphysical.**\
\
Paul is not writing a treatise on the nature of Christ. He is exhorting the Philippians (vv. 3-4) to walk in humility \'97 *"Let nothing be done through strife or vainglory; but in lowliness of mind let each esteem others better than himself."*\
\
**(2) The example he cites is Christ's mind-set** \'97 **\uc0\u964 \u959 \u8166 \u964 \u959  \u966 \u961 \u959 \u957 \u949 \u8150 \u964 \u949 ** \'97 *"have this mind-set."* The Philippians are called to imitate what Christ *did* in His attitude and action.\
\
**(3) This has decisive implications for interpretation.** If Paul's primary intent were to establish metaphysical doctrine, he would have written differently. He is presenting Christ's example \'97 His self-emptying, His humility, His obedience \'97 as the pattern for our own conduct.\
\
**Per our maxims, this framing must govern our reading of vv. 6-8.** We must not press this passage beyond what Paul intends it to establish. It is an *ethical exhortation grounded in the Son's actual humbling* \'97 not a philosophical treatise on essence or substance.\
\
---\
\
# **PHILIPPIANS 2:6 \'97 THE MOST CONTESTED VERSE**\
\
### **Greek:**\
*"\uc0\u8003 \u962  \u7952 \u957  \u956 \u959 \u961 \u966 \u8135  \u920 \u949 \u959 \u8166  \u8017 \u960 \u940 \u961 \u967 \u969 \u957  \u959 \u8016 \u967  \u7937 \u961 \u960 \u945 \u947 \u956 \u8056 \u957  \u7969 \u947 \u942 \u963 \u945 \u964 \u959  \u964 \u8056  \u949 \u7990 \u957 \u945 \u953  \u7988 \u963 \u945  \u920 \u949 \u8183 ,"*\
\
### **1599 Geneva:**\
*"Who being in the form of God, thought it no robbery to be equal with God:"*\
\
---\
\
Let me now examine each phrase with the greatest care.\
\
---\
\
## **Phrase 1: \uc0\u8003 \u962  \u7952 \u957  \u956 \u959 \u961 \u966 \u8135  \u920 \u949 \u959 \u8166  \u8017 \u960 \u940 \u961 \u967 \u969 \u957 **\
\
*"Who, existing in the form of God..."*\
\
### The Greek word **\uc0\u956 \u959 \u961 \u966 \u942 **\
\
**\uc0\u956 \u959 \u961 \u966 \u942 ** means *"form, outward expression, that which reflects the essential nature or state of a thing."* It is distinct from **\u963 \u967 \u8134 \u956 \u945 ** (outward appearance, fashion) which Paul will use in v. 8.\
\
**Honest lexical range:**\
- **\uc0\u956 \u959 \u961 \u966 \u942 ** can indicate the outward form that reveals the inner essence.\
- In some Greek usage, it approaches the meaning of essential nature.\
- It does not, however, necessarily mean "identical essence" in the metaphysical sense later theologians pressed it to bear.\
\
### The Participle **\uc0\u8017 \u960 \u940 \u961 \u967 \u969 \u957 **\
\
**\uc0\u8017 \u960 \u940 \u961 \u967 \u969 \u957 ** is a present active participle of **\u8017 \u960 \u940 \u961 \u967 \u969 ** \'97 *"to exist, to subsist, to be in a state or condition."* It can indicate:\
- Continuing existence in a particular state.\
- Being characterized by a particular condition.\
\
The participle does not by itself establish *eternal* pre-existence in a specific metaphysical sense \'97 it establishes that Christ *was existing in the form of God* prior to what follows.\
\
### **\uc0\u7952 \u957  \u956 \u959 \u961 \u966 \u8135  \u920 \u949 \u959 \u8166 **\
\
*"In the form of God."*\
\
Honest questions we must ask:\
- Does this phrase mean Christ *was* God in essence?\
- Does it mean Christ *was* the visible manifestation/outward form of God?\
- Does it mean Christ *bore the character and nature of God* prior to His incarnation?\
\
**Per our maxims, we must not overstate.** The phrase itself establishes that Christ, in His pre-incarnate state, existed in the **form of God** \'97 but the precise metaphysical weight this phrase must bear is contested even among honest interpreters.\
\
### What does the whole counsel establish alongside this?\
\
**Cross-witnesses of pre-incarnate state:**\
\
- **John 17:5** \'97 Jesus prays to the Father: *"glorify Me with the glory I had with You before the world was."* This is direct testimony from Jesus Himself that He shared glory with the Father before creation.\
\
- **John 8:58** \'97 *"Before Abraham was, I am."* Direct claim to existence before Abraham.\
\
- **John 1:1-3** \'97 the Word was in the beginning with God, and all things came to be through Him. (Note: we handled this text carefully under Part D \'97 Reading A and Reading B both permitted by the grammar.)\
\
- **Heb 1:2-3** \'97 the Son through whom the Father made the ages; the radiance of His glory and exact impression of His being.\
\
- **Col 1:15-17** \'97 the image of the invisible God, firstborn of all creation, through whom all things were made.\
\
**These cross-witnesses establish, by multiple direct testimonies including Jesus' own words:**\
- **The Son existed personally before His incarnation.**\
- **He shared glory with the Father.**\
- **He was the agent through whom the Father created.**\
- **He bore the Father's image and radiated His glory.**\
\
**Therefore "in the form of God" in Phil 2:6 is best understood** as consistent with these established truths \'97 **the Son, in His pre-incarnate state, existed in the divine mode of being, sharing the Father's glory, bearing His image, radiating His nature.**\
\
**What "in the form of God" does *not* by itself establish** (per our maxims):\
- The specific metaphysical claim that the Son is "of one substance" with the Father in the post-Nicene sense.\
- That the Son is co-equal in the sense that would displace the Father's supremacy (which 1 Cor 15:28 explicitly forbids).\
- That "form of God" and "equal with God" mean identity of Person with the Father.\
\
**The honest position:** The Son existed in the form/glory/divine mode of the Father before His incarnation \'97 a truth established by multiple direct witnesses. The precise metaphysical formulation is not decided by this phrase alone.\
\
---\
\
## **Phrase 2: \uc0\u959 \u8016 \u967  \u7937 \u961 \u960 \u945 \u947 \u956 \u8056 \u957  \u7969 \u947 \u942 \u963 \u945 \u964 \u959  \u964 \u8056  \u949 \u7990 \u957 \u945 \u953  \u7988 \u963 \u945  \u920 \u949 \u8183 **\
\
*"Did not consider being equal with God something to be grasped."*\
\
**This is the most textually and translationally contested phrase in the entire passage.**\
\
### The word **\uc0\u7937 \u961 \u960 \u945 \u947 \u956 \u972 \u962 **\
\
**\uc0\u7937 \u961 \u960 \u945 \u947 \u956 \u972 \u962 ** is a rare word (this may be its only NT occurrence). It comes from **\u7937 \u961 \u960 \u940 \u950 \u969 ** \'97 *"to seize, to snatch, to plunder, to take by force."*\
\
**The word can mean:**\
- **(A)** A thing to be seized/grasped after (something one does not yet have and reaches for).\
- **(B)** A thing already possessed and clung to (something one holds tightly and refuses to release).\
\
**These are two very different meanings**, and the translation depends on which is chosen.\
\
### The Two Major Readings\
\
**Reading 1 (traditional / Geneva):** *"He thought it no robbery to be equal with God."*\
- Sense: He already was equal with God, and did not consider this equality a "robbery" \'97 i.e., something illegitimately held. He rightfully possessed it, but did not cling to it.\
- This reading supports the traditional metaphysical claim.\
\
**Reading 2 (many modern translations):** *"He did not consider equality with God something to be grasped."*\
- Sense: He did not seek to seize or clutch at equality with God \'97 unlike Adam, who did seize at being like God (Gen 3:5).\
- This reading emphasizes the contrast with Adam and highlights the humility.\
\
**Reading 3 (a variant of 1):** *"He did not consider being equal with God as something to be held onto / exploited for advantage."*\
- Sense: He possessed a divine mode of being but did not use it for His own benefit or self-preservation.\
\
### Honest Assessment Per Our Maxims\
\
**Per maxim #1 (let God be true):** The Greek is genuinely difficult, and we must not force a reading beyond what the text can bear.\
\
**Per maxim #2 (two or more witnesses):** Whatever we conclude here must cohere with the whole counsel. If our reading of Phil 2:6 contradicts John 14:28 (*"the Father is greater than I"*), or 1 Cor 15:28 (*"the Son also Himself shall be subject to Him"*), or Mark 13:32 (*"the Son does not know"*), or the Shema itself \'97 we have read too much into this verse.\
\
**Per maxim #3 (ignoring accretions):** Post-Nicene tradition has often read this verse as establishing "co-equal substance" \'97 a metaphysical formulation the text does not deliver in those terms. We must set this aside.\
\
### The Most Defensible Honest Reading\
\
**The Son, existing in the divine form/glory, did not treat that state as something to be seized upon or clung to for His own advantage.** Instead \'97 as the following verses will show \'97 He emptied Himself. Whether we translate *"thought it no robbery"* (Geneva) or *"did not consider something to be grasped,"* the essential point is the same: **He did not hold onto His divine form for His own benefit; He released it for the sake of us.**\
\
**\uc0\u964 \u8056  \u949 \u7990 \u957 \u945 \u953  \u7988 \u963 \u945  \u920 \u949 \u8183 ** \'97 *"the being equal with God."*\
\
**\uc0\u7988 \u963 \u945 ** is neuter plural \'97 literally, *"equal things"* or *"in an equal manner."* It is an adverbial usage. The phrase means *"the being in equality with God"* or *"to exist on equal terms with God."*\
\
**Per our maxims, this must be honestly held alongside:**\
- **John 14:28:** *"the Father is greater than I."*\
- **John 5:19:** *"the Son can do nothing of Himself."*\
- **1 Cor 15:28:** the Son subjected to the Father.\
- **Mark 13:32:** the Son does not know what the Father knows.\
\
**How to reconcile?** The Son's *equality* in Phil 2:6 refers to His pre-incarnate mode of existence in the divine glory \'97 sharing what belonged uniquely to the Father as the Father's Beloved Only-Begotten. This does not mean identity of Person or metaphysical co-equality in the post-Nicene sense; it means the shared divine glory the Son had with the Father before the world was (John 17:5).\
\
