# **PHILIPPIANS 2:7 \'97 THE SELF-EMPTYING**\
\
### **Greek:**\
*"\uc0\u7936 \u955 \u955 \u8048  \u7953 \u945 \u965 \u964 \u8056 \u957  \u7952 \u954 \u941 \u957 \u969 \u963 \u949 \u957  \u956 \u959 \u961 \u966 \u8052 \u957  \u948 \u959 \u973 \u955 \u959 \u965  \u955 \u945 \u946 \u974 \u957 , \u7952 \u957  \u8001 \u956 \u959 \u953 \u974 \u956 \u945 \u964 \u953  \u7936 \u957 \u952 \u961 \u974 \u960 \u969 \u957  \u947 \u949 \u957 \u972 \u956 \u949 \u957 \u959 \u962 \'b7"*\
\
### **1599 Geneva:**\
*"But he made himself of no reputation, and took on him the form of a servant, and was made like unto men..."*\
\
---\
\
## **Phrase 1: \uc0\u7936 \u955 \u955 \u8048  \u7953 \u945 \u965 \u964 \u8056 \u957  \u7952 \u954 \u941 \u957 \u969 \u963 \u949 \u957 **\
\
*"But He emptied Himself."*\
\
### The Word **\uc0\u7952 \u954 \u941 \u957 \u969 \u963 \u949 \u957 **\
\
**\uc0\u7952 \u954 \u941 \u957 \u969 \u963 \u949 \u957 ** is aorist active of **\u954 \u949 \u957 \u972 \u969 ** \'97 *"to empty, to make empty, to make of no effect, to make of no reputation."*\
\
**This is the famous kenosis passage.** The Son "emptied Himself."\
\
### The Critical Question: Emptied Himself of *What*?\
\
The Greek does not specify. The verb is used **absolutely** \'97 *"He emptied Himself"* \'97 without a direct object.\
\
**Various theological readings have been offered:**\
- He emptied Himself of divine attributes (some kenotic theologians).\
- He emptied Himself of the *use* of divine attributes.\
- He emptied Himself of the divine glory / mode of existence.\
- He simply "made Himself of no reputation" (the Geneva's rendering \'97 treating it as a self-humbling, not a literal emptying of essence).\
\
### Honest Assessment Per Our Maxims\
\
**Per our maxims, we must not press this beyond what Scripture as a whole establishes.**\
\
**What Scripture as a whole establishes:**\
\
1. **The Son truly became flesh** (John 1:14) \'97 a real transition into human existence.\
2. **The Son was truly hungry, weary, tempted, suffering** (Matt 4:2; John 4:6; Heb 4:15; Isa 53).\
3. **The Son did not know what the Father knew** (Mark 13:32).\
4. **The Son said "the Father is greater than I"** (John 14:28).\
5. **The Son did nothing of Himself** (John 5:19; 8:28).\
6. **The Son operated by the Father's Spirit indwelling Him** (John 14:10; Acts 10:38).\
7. **The Son received back His glory after His resurrection and exaltation** (John 17:5; Phil 2:9-11).\
\
**The Geneva's rendering \'97 "he made himself of no reputation"** \'97 captures the essential meaning: **the Son gave up the outward display of His divine glory, took on the humble form of a servant, and lived as a Man in dependence on the Father's Spirit.**\
\
He did not cease to be who He is. He truly took on humanity. He truly walked in dependence, in weakness, in obedience, unto death. **This is the great humbling.**\
\
---\
\
## **Phrase 2: \uc0\u956 \u959 \u961 \u966 \u8052 \u957  \u948 \u959 \u973 \u955 \u959 \u965  \u955 \u945 \u946 \u974 \u957 **\
\
*"Taking the form of a servant."*\
\
**Same word \uc0\u956 \u959 \u961 \u966 \u942  as in v. 6** \'97 deliberate contrast. Previously **\u7952 \u957  \u956 \u959 \u961 \u966 \u8135  \u920 \u949 \u959 \u8166 ** (*in the form of God*), now **\u956 \u959 \u961 \u966 \u8052 \u957  \u948 \u959 \u973 \u955 \u959 \u965 ** (*form of a servant*).\
\
**\uc0\u955 \u945 \u946 \u974 \u957 ** \'97 aorist active participle \'97 *"taking, receiving."* The Son actively took on this new mode of being.\
\
**\uc0\u948 \u959 \u973 \u955 \u959 \u965 ** \'97 *"of a servant / slave."* Not just a lowly human, but a **servant/slave.** The Son entered the world in the lowest social station.\
\
**This echoes Isaiah 52:13-53:12** \'97 the Suffering Servant. **\uc0\u960 \u945 \u8150 \u962 ** (*servant/child*) is the very word used of Jesus in the apostolic preaching (Acts 3:13, 26; 4:27, 30). We examined this fully in our study of Isaiah 53.\
\
**The Son's incarnation was into servanthood, not into royalty.** He entered as a servant of the Father to accomplish the Father's will.\
\
---\
\
## **Phrase 3: \uc0\u7952 \u957  \u8001 \u956 \u959 \u953 \u974 \u956 \u945 \u964 \u953  \u7936 \u957 \u952 \u961 \u974 \u960 \u969 \u957  \u947 \u949 \u957 \u972 \u956 \u949 \u957 \u959 \u962 **\
\
*"Being made in the likeness of men."*\
\
**\uc0\u8001 \u956 \u959 \u943 \u969 \u956 \u945 ** \'97 *"likeness, similitude."* The Son took on the **likeness** of men. He truly became human \'97 sharing our nature. Yet the word **\u8001 \u956 \u959 \u943 \u969 \u956 \u945 ** carefully preserves that He was **like** men in every respect that mattered for His mission, while being without sin (Heb 4:15).\
\
**\uc0\u947 \u949 \u957 \u972 \u956 \u949 \u957 \u959 \u962 ** \'97 aorist middle participle of **\u947 \u943 \u957 \u959 \u956 \u945 \u953 ** \'97 *"becoming, coming into being, being made."* Same word used in John 1:14 (*"the Word became flesh"* \'97 **\u963 \u8048 \u961 \u958  \u7952 \u947 \u941 \u957 \u949 \u964 \u959 **).\
\
**The Son truly became a man.** This is the doctrine of the true incarnation.\
\
