# **THE GRAND SYNTHESIS \'97 WHAT PHIL 2:5-11 ESTABLISHES**\
\
Per our maxims, let us now state honestly what this passage does and does not establish.\
\
## **What the Passage Firmly Establishes**\
\
**(1) The Son truly existed before His incarnation** \'97 in the "form of God" \'97 sharing the divine glory and mode of being with the Father. (Confirmed by John 17:5; John 8:58; Col 1:15-17; Heb 1:3.)\
\
**(2) The Son voluntarily humbled Himself** \'97 took on human form, became a servant, was obedient unto the death of the cross. This is the great humbling.\
\
**(3) The Son did not exalt Himself; the Father exalted Him** \'97 the Father is the actor throughout the exaltation.\
\
**(4) The Father graciously gave the Son the Name above every name.** The Name was **given** \'97 not innately possessed independent of the Father.\
\
**(5) Every knee shall bow to Jesus** \'97 but this bowing is to the glory of God the Father.\
\
**(6) The passage's primary purpose is ethical** \'97 Paul is calling the Philippians to imitate the Son's humility. It is not primarily a metaphysical treatise.\
\
## **What the Passage Does *Not* Establish (per our maxims)**\
\
**(1) The passage does not establish** the post-Nicene metaphysical formulation of "co-equal, co-eternal, of one substance." The Greek does not deliver this precise doctrine.\
\
**(2) The passage does not overthrow** the Father's supremacy. Every element of the passage preserves the Father as the exalter, the giver, and the ultimate end of glory.\
\
**(3) The passage does not establish** identity between the Father and the Son in Person. The passage carefully distinguishes: the Father exalts; the Son is exalted. Two distinct figures throughout.\
\
**(4) The passage does not establish** that the Son is "equal with God" in the sense that would displace the Father as the One God. Even at His highest exaltation, Jesus is confessed as Lord *unto the glory of God the Father.*\
\
## **How This Passage Fits Our Established Foundation**\
\
Everything in Phil 2:5-11 is entirely consistent with the foundation we have laid throughout our study:\
\
- **The Father is the One God** \'97 He is the one who exalts, who gives the Name, whose glory is the ultimate end.\
- **The Son is the Father's Beloved** \'97 pre-existent in glory, willingly humbled, obediently dying, gloriously exalted.\
- **The Son's exaltation is by the Father's grant** \'97 not by inherent right independent of the Father.\
- **The Son's Lordship is unto the Father's glory** \'97 the Son's supremacy always terminates in the Father's honor.\
\
**Phil 2:5-11 does not undo our foundation; it beautifully fulfills it.**\
