# **A CRITICAL OBSERVATION \'97 HOW THIS PASSAGE HAS BEEN MISHANDLED**\
\
Per maxim #3, we must honestly note how post-Nicene tradition has often pressed this passage beyond what it establishes.\
\
**The traditional Trinitarian reading of Phil 2:6-8** often argues:\
- *"In the form of God"* = *"of one substance with the Father"* \'97 a claim the Greek does not make.\
- *"Equal with God"* = *"co-equal Person of the Godhead"* \'97 a claim the passage does not make in these terms.\
- The Son's exaltation is not really an exaltation to something new but merely a "revelation" of what He always was \'97 undermining the actual movement of the text.\
\
**Per our maxims, we set this aside.**\
\
The passage says what it says: **The Son existed in the form of God, humbled Himself, and was exalted by the Father.** The exaltation is real, not merely revelatory. The Name is given, not innately possessed. The bowing is unto the Father's glory.\
\
**The passage is not a metaphysical proof-text for co-equal substance;** it is a beautiful account of the Son's actual humbling and the Father's actual exalting \'97 and it is offered as the pattern for our own humility.\
\
