\
### **Phrase 2: \uc0\u954 \u945 \u8054  \u8001  \u923 \u972 \u947 \u959 \u962  \u7974 \u957  \u960 \u961 \u8056 \u962  \u964 \u8056 \u957  \u920 \u949 \u972 \u957 ** \'97 *"and the Word was with God"*\
\
**\uc0\u960 \u961 \u8056 \u962  \u964 \u8056 \u957  \u920 \u949 \u972 \u957 ** \'97 *"toward / in relation to / face-to-face with the God."*\
\
The preposition **\uc0\u960 \u961 \u972 \u962 ** with the accusative denotes **orientation, relation, presence-toward.** The Word was **facing** God, in relational presence with Him.\
\
**\uc0\u964 \u8056 \u957  \u920 \u949 \u972 \u957 ** \'97 with the definite article \'97 clearly refers to **the Father** (as we established under John 17:3; 1 Cor 8:6).\
\
**Under your proposal:**\
\
The Word \'97 the Son begotten of the Father in Spirit \'97 existed **in personal, face-to-face communion** with the Father before creation. Two distinct figures in living relation. This is precisely what John 17:5 confirms: *"the glory I had with You before the world was."*\
\
**The Word was with God \'97 meaning the Only-Begotten Son was in living, personal communion with the Father in the Spirit-realm before creation.**\
\
---\
\
### **Phrase 3: \uc0\u954 \u945 \u8054  \u920 \u949 \u8056 \u962  \u7974 \u957  \u8001  \u923 \u972 \u947 \u959 \u962 ** \'97 *"and the Word was God"*\
\
**This is the most contested clause.** We must handle it with the utmost care under our maxims.\
\
**Critical Greek grammar:**\
\
- **\uc0\u920 \u949 \u972 \u962 ** is **anarthrous** (no article) \'97 placed first in the clause for emphasis.\
- **\uc0\u8001  \u923 \u972 \u947 \u959 \u962 ** has the article \'97 clearly the subject.\
\
The word order is: *"and God was the Word"* \'97 but grammatically, the subject is **\uc0\u8001  \u923 \u972 \u947 \u959 \u962 ** (with the article), and the predicate is **\u920 \u949 \u972 \u962 ** (without the article).\
\
**Why does John write anarthrous \uc0\u920 \u949 \u972 \u962  here?**\
\
If John had written **\uc0\u954 \u945 \u8054  \u8001  \u920 \u949 \u8056 \u962  \u7974 \u957  \u8001  \u923 \u972 \u947 \u959 \u962 ** \'97 *"and THE God was the Word"* \'97 this would identify the Word as *identical* to *the God* just mentioned (the Father). This would be a flat contradiction of v. 1b (the Word was WITH the God) and would collapse Father and Son into one Person.\
\
**John deliberately avoided this.** By writing anarthrous **\uc0\u920 \u949 \u972 \u962 **, he indicates something different: **the Word was of divine quality / nature / mode of being** \'97 but not identical Person with **\u8001  \u920 \u949 \u972 \u962 ** of v. 1b.\
\
**Under your proposal:**\
\
The Word \'97 the Only-Begotten Son begotten of the Father in Spirit \'97 shared **the Father's Spirit-nature / divine mode of being.** He was **\uc0\u920 \u949 \u972 \u962  in quality** (divine, of the Father's essence, sharing His Spirit-nature) \'97 because He was the Father's Only-Begotten, brought forth from the Father's own being.\
\
This is not identity of Person (v. 1b prevents that reading). This is not "co-equal substance" in the post-Nicene sense (which the text does not deliver). **This is the shared Spirit-nature of the Only-Begotten with the Father.**\
\
**Some careful translations reflect this:**\
- *"the Word was divine"* (some translations) \'97 captures qualitative sense.\
- *"the Word was God"* (Geneva, most traditional translations) \'97 captures the qualitative claim but risks collapsing Persons.\
\
**The Geneva's rendering is defensible** if understood in the qualitative sense \'97 the Word was of God's nature \'97 not identifying Him as Person-identical to the Father.\
\
**Under your proposal, this resolves beautifully:** The Word shared the Father's Spirit-nature as the Only-Begotten, brought forth from Him \'97 genuinely divine in mode of being, yet distinct in Person from the Father with whom He was face-to-face.\
\
---\
\
### **Phrase 4: \uc0\u959 \u8023 \u964 \u959 \u962  \u7974 \u957  \u7952 \u957  \u7936 \u961 \u967 \u8135  \u960 \u961 \u8056 \u962  \u964 \u8056 \u957  \u920 \u949 \u972 \u957 ** \'97 *"This same was in the beginning with God"*\
\
Repetition for emphasis. **The Word was with the Father at the beginning.** Two distinct figures. Personal communion in the Spirit-realm before creation.\
\
---\
\
### **Phrase 5: \uc0\u960 \u940 \u957 \u964 \u945  \u948 \u953 \u8125  \u945 \u8016 \u964 \u959 \u8166  \u7952 \u947 \u941 \u957 \u949 \u964 \u959 ** \'97 *"All things came to be through Him"*\
\
Under your proposal:\
\
The Father, who is Spirit (John 4:24), created all things **through** His Only-Begotten Son \'97 the Word \'97 who existed with Him in the Spirit-realm before creation. This is exactly the pattern of 1 Cor 8:6: *"from the Father are all things... through the Lord Jesus Christ are all things."*\
\
The Father is Source (**\uc0\u7952 \u958  \u959 \u8023  \u964 \u8048  \u960 \u940 \u957 \u964 \u945 **); the Son is Agent (**\u948 \u953 \u8125  \u959 \u8023  \u964 \u8048  \u960 \u940 \u957 \u964 \u945 **). **The Son as the Father's Word \'97 His creative Utterance, His Spirit-begotten Only-Son \'97 was the means through which the Father brought all things into being.**\
\
This coheres with Ps 32:6 LXX (33:6 MT) \'97 *"By the Word of the LORD were the heavens made, and all the host of them by the breath of His mouth."* The Word (\uc0\u923 \u972 \u947 \u959 \u962 ) and the Breath (Spirit) \'97 both belong to the LORD (the Father), and through both He creates.\
\
**Under your proposal, John 1:1-3 reads coherently:**\
\
*"In the beginning, the Only-Begotten Son \'97 the Word \'97 was already existing. He was in face-to-face communion with the Father. He shared the Father's Spirit-nature as the Only-Begotten. He was there at the beginning with the Father. All things came into being through Him \'97 the Father's Word, His Spirit-begotten Agent \'97 and without Him nothing came into being that has come into being."*\
\
**This reading:**\
- Honors the Greek grammar (anarthrous \uc0\u920 \u949 \u972 \u962  preserves distinction from \u8001  \u920 \u949 \u972 \u962  of v. 1b).\
- Coheres with John 17:5 (glory with the Father before the world was).\
- Coheres with Heb 10:5 (a body was prepared for Him later).\
- Coheres with 1 Cor 8:6 (Father as Source, Son as Agent).\
- Preserves the Shema (Father as the One God).\
- Illuminates "only-begotten" (John 1:14, 18).\
- Requires no post-Nicene metaphysical accretions.\
\
