\
## **Careful Examination**\
\
### **The Original Psalm**\
\
Psalm 45 is a **royal wedding psalm** \'97 celebrating the king and his bride. It is applied by the author of Hebrews to Christ as the Messianic King.\
\
**Two critical observations:**\
\
**(1)** The psalm addresses the king as **\uc0\u8001  \u920 \u949 \u972 \u962 ** \'97 *"O God"* \'97 a striking address.\
\
**(2)** In the very next verse, the same King is told: **\uc0\u7956 \u967 \u961 \u953 \u963 \u941 \u957  \u963 \u949  \u8001  \u920 \u949 \u972 \u962  \u8001  \u920 \u949 \u972 \u962  \u963 \u959 \u965 ** \'97 *"God, even Thy God, hath anointed Thee."*\
\
**This is the decisive contextual observation.** The One addressed as **\uc0\u8001  \u920 \u949 \u972 \u962 ** in v. 6 is Himself said to have **a God who anointed Him** in v. 7.\
\
**The King has a God \'97 the God who anointed Him.**\
\
---\
\
## **Examining Under Our Proposal**\
\
**Under your proposal:**\
\
The Son, as the Only-Begotten begotten of the Father in Spirit, shares the Father's Spirit-nature. In this shared divine nature and by virtue of being the Father's Only-Begotten who bears the Father's Name, He can legitimately be addressed as **\uc0\u8001  \u920 \u949 \u972 \u962 ** \'97 *"O God"* \'97 because He bears the divine nature.\
\
**But \'97 and this is critical \'97 the same passage immediately identifies the Father as *"God, even Thy God"* who anointed the Son.**\
\
**The Father is the Son's God.**\
\
This is entirely consistent with:\
- **John 20:17** \'97 Jesus after the resurrection: *"I ascend unto My Father, and your Father; and to My God, and your God."*\
- **Eph 1:17** \'97 *"the God of our Lord Jesus Christ, the Father of glory."*\
- **1 Pet 1:3** \'97 *"the God and Father of our Lord Jesus Christ."*\
- **Rev 3:12** \'97 Jesus speaks of *"My God"* four times.\
\
**The Son has a God. That God is the Father.**\
\
**How, then, is the Son addressed as *"O God"* in Ps 45:6 / Heb 1:8?**\
\
Under your proposal:\
\
The Son, as the Father's Only-Begotten sharing His Spirit-nature and bearing His Name (Ex 23:21), can be addressed with divine titles that reflect His shared nature with the Father. He is genuinely divine \'97 begotten of the Father's own Spirit-essence.\
\
**But this does not make Him identical Person with the Father.** The Father remains His God \'97 the One who anointed Him, sent Him, exalted Him, and to whom He ultimately delivers all things (1 Cor 15:24-28).\
\
**The address "O God" reflects the Son's shared Spirit-nature with the Father. The following clause "God, even Thy God, hath anointed Thee" preserves the Father's supremacy.**\
\
**Under our maxims, this reading:**\
- Honors the text (which does address the Son as "O God").\
- Preserves the Father's supremacy (which the immediate context establishes).\
- Coheres with the whole counsel (the Father as the Son's God throughout the NT).\
- Requires no post-Nicene metaphysical formulation to hold together.\
\
**The Son can bear divine titles as the Father's Only-Begotten who shares His Spirit-nature and bears His Name \'97 without displacing the Father as the One God who is Himself the Son's God.**\
\
---\
\
## **A Careful Note on Textual Variants**\
\
There is a translational question in Heb 1:8. The Greek **\uc0\u8001  \u952 \u961 \u972 \u957 \u959 \u962  \u963 \u959 \u965 , \u8001  \u920 \u949 \u972 \u962 ** can be read in two ways:\
\
**Reading 1 (most translations, Geneva):** *"Thy throne, O God, is forever and ever"* \'97 treating **\uc0\u8001  \u920 \u949 \u972 \u962 ** as a vocative address to the Son.\
\
**Reading 2 (some scholars):** *"God is Thy throne forever and ever"* \'97 treating **\uc0\u8001  \u920 \u949 \u972 \u962 ** as the subject.\
\
**Under Reading 2, the theological weight shifts significantly** \'97 the Son is not being addressed as God, but the Father is being called the Son's throne (His foundation, His authority).\
\
**Per our maxims, we do not need to force Reading 2.** Reading 1 (the traditional and most natural rendering) can be honestly held under our proposal \'97 the Son bears divine titles as the Only-Begotten sharing the Father's Spirit-nature, while the Father remains His God.\
\
**Either reading, the essential truth is preserved:** the Son is uniquely honored, and the Father is His God.\
\
