## **The Grammatical Question**\
\
Both verses have the same construction: **\uc0\u964 \u959 \u8166  [\u956 \u949 \u947 \u940 \u955 \u959 \u965 ] \u920 \u949 \u959 \u8166  \u954 \u945 \u8054  \u931 \u969 \u964 \u8134 \u961 \u959 \u962  [\u7969 \u956 \u8182 \u957 ] \u7992 \u951 \u963 \u959 \u8166  \u935 \u961 \u953 \u963 \u964 \u959 \u8166 .**\
\
The question is whether this construction:\
\
**Reading A:** Refers to **one Person** \'97 *"our great God and Savior, Jesus Christ"* (Jesus is called both God and Savior).\
\
**Reading B:** Refers to **two Persons** \'97 *"the great God, and our Savior Jesus Christ"* (the Father is called God, and Jesus is called Savior separately).\
\
The Geneva translates Titus 2:13 in a way that allows Reading B: *"the mighty God, and of our Saviour Jesus Christ"* \'97 treating them as two.\
\
The Geneva translates 2 Pet 1:1 in a way that could allow Reading A: *"our God and Saviour Jesus Christ"* \'97 but the "of our" is somewhat ambiguous.\
\
---\
\
## **The Granville Sharp Rule**\
\
Some Greek grammarians argue (per what is called "Granville Sharp's rule") that when two nouns are joined by **\uc0\u954 \u945 \u943 ** with the article only before the first noun (**\u964 \u959 \u8166  \u913  \u954 \u945 \u8054  \u914 **), they refer to the same person. Under this rule, both Titus 2:13 and 2 Pet 1:1 would be Reading A.\
\
However, this rule has exceptions and is not universally accepted as strict. The construction can bear either reading depending on context.\
\
