# **PASSAGE E \'97 ISAIAH 9:6 LXX**\
\
This is one of the most theologically weighted OT passages, and it is critical to compare the LXX and Hebrew readings \'97 per our maxim to follow the LXX that Jesus and the Apostles used.\
\
## **The Hebrew Reading (Reflected in Geneva)**\
\
### **1599 Geneva (Isa 9:6):**\
*"For unto us a child is born, and unto us a Son is given: and the government is upon his shoulder, and he shall call his name, Wonderful, Counsellor, The mighty God, The everlasting Father, The prince of peace."*\
\
The Hebrew reading (as translated in the Geneva) contains four (or five) titles:\
- Wonderful, Counsellor\
- The mighty God (**\uc0\u1488 \u1461 \u1500  \u1490 \u1460 \u1468 \u1489 \u1468 \u1493 \u1465 \u1512 ** \'97 El Gibbor)\
- The everlasting Father (**\uc0\u1488 \u1458 \u1489 \u1460 \u1497 \u1506 \u1463 \u1491 ** \'97 Abi-Ad)\
- The prince of peace\
\
## **The LXX Reading**\
\
### **Greek (Isa 9:5-6 LXX \'97 note the LXX numbering differs):**\
*"\uc0\u8005 \u964 \u953  \u960 \u945 \u953 \u948 \u943 \u959 \u957  \u7952 \u947 \u949 \u957 \u942 \u952 \u951  \u7969 \u956 \u8150 \u957 , \u965 \u7985 \u8056 \u962  \u954 \u945 \u8054  \u7952 \u948 \u972 \u952 \u951  \u7969 \u956 \u8150 \u957 , \u959 \u8023  \u7969  \u7936 \u961 \u967 \u8052  \u7952 \u947 \u949 \u957 \u942 \u952 \u951  \u7952 \u960 \u8054  \u964 \u959 \u8166  \u8036 \u956 \u959 \u965  \u945 \u8016 \u964 \u959 \u8166 , \u954 \u945 \u8054  \u954 \u945 \u955 \u949 \u8150 \u964 \u945 \u953  \u964 \u8056  \u8004 \u957 \u959 \u956 \u945  \u945 \u8016 \u964 \u959 \u8166  \u924 \u949 \u947 \u940 \u955 \u951 \u962  \u946 \u959 \u965 \u955 \u8134 \u962  \u7940 \u947 \u947 \u949 \u955 \u959 \u962 \'b7 \u7952 \u947 \u8060  \u947 \u8048 \u961  \u7940 \u958 \u969  \u949 \u7984 \u961 \u942 \u957 \u951 \u957  \u7952 \u960 \u8054  \u964 \u959 \u8058 \u962  \u7940 \u961 \u967 \u959 \u957 \u964 \u945 \u962 , \u949 \u7984 \u961 \u942 \u957 \u951 \u957  \u954 \u945 \u8054  \u8017 \u947 \u943 \u949 \u953 \u945 \u957  \u945 \u8016 \u964 \u8183 ."*\
\
### **Literal translation of the LXX:**\
*"For a child is born to us, and a son is given to us, whose government is upon His shoulder, and His name is called Messenger of Great Counsel; for I will bring peace upon the rulers, peace and health to him."*\
\
---\
\
## **Observation of Decisive Importance for Our Maxims**\
\
**The LXX reading is remarkably different from the Hebrew.**\
\
**The LXX simply calls the Child: "Messenger of Great Counsel" (\uc0\u924 \u949 \u947 \u940 \u955 \u951 \u962  \u946 \u959 \u965 \u955 \u8134 \u962  \u7940 \u947 \u947 \u949 \u955 \u959 \u962 ).**\
\
**The LXX does NOT include:**\
- "Mighty God"\
- "Everlasting Father"\
\
**These titles are present in the Hebrew but absent from the LXX** that Jesus and the Apostles used.\
\
---\
\
## **Per Our Maxims**\
\
**Per maxim #1 (let God be true):** We follow the God-breathed Word \'97 and per our commitment throughout this study, we prioritize the LXX that Jesus and the Apostles used.\
\
**Per maxim #3 (ignoring accretions):** The Hebrew reading with "Mighty God, Everlasting Father" \'97 as translated in the Geneva \'97 is what the Masoretic tradition preserved. But the LXX (translated by Jewish scholars centuries before Christ) reads differently.\
\
**We should not build major Christological doctrines on the Hebrew reading of Isa 9:6 alone** \'97 because the LXX (which Jesus and His Apostles used) does not confirm these specific titles for the Child.\
\
---\
\
## **What the LXX Does Confirm**\
\
The LXX still presents this Child as:\
- **The Messenger of Great Counsel** \'97 the One bearing the Father's counsel and authority.\
- **The One whose government is on His shoulder** \'97 bearing supreme rule.\
- **The One through whom peace comes** \'97 the Prince of Peace, effectively.\
\
**This is coherent with everything we have established:**\
- The Son is the Father's Messenger \'97 bearing the Father's Name and authority (John 5:43; Ex 23:21).\
- The Son bears the government / kingdom given to Him by the Father (Dan 7:13-14; Matt 28:18).\
- The Son is the Prince of Peace \'97 through whom the Father brings peace.\
\
**No metaphysical claim of the Son being "Mighty God, Everlasting Father" is required from the LXX text.**\
\
---\
\
## **A Note on the Hebrew "Everlasting Father" (\uc0\u1488 \u1458 \u1489 \u1460 \u1497 \u1506 \u1463 \u1491 )**\
\
Even in the Hebrew tradition, the title **"Everlasting Father"** applied to the Son has been a source of theological difficulty for post-Nicene tradition. If the Son is called "Father," how does He remain distinct from the Father?\
\
**Answer:** The title **"Father"** in the Hebrew idiom can mean *"originator, founder, source"* \'97 as in *"father of many nations"* (Abraham), *"father of the fatherless"* (God), etc. It does not necessarily mean identical Person with **the** Father.\
\
**"Everlasting Father"** in Isa 9:6 (Hebrew) may mean **"Father of the age to come"** \'97 the One who founds and sustains the everlasting age of the kingdom. This does not collapse Him into the Person of God the Father.\
\
**But since our maxim prioritizes the LXX, we need not resolve this here.** The LXX reading (\uc0\u924 \u949 \u947 \u940 \u955 \u951 \u962  \u946 \u959 \u965 \u955 \u8134 \u962  \u7940 \u947 \u947 \u949 \u955 \u959 \u962 ) does not contain this title, and per our discipline, we let the LXX guide us.\
\
**Under our maxims, Isa 9:6 does not present a metaphysical challenge to our framework** \'97 because the LXX reading is consistent with the Name-authority framework you have proposed.\
\
