# **PASSAGE G \'97 1 TIMOTHY 3:16**\
\
## **The Textual Variant**\
\
### **The Textus Receptus (followed by the Geneva) reads:**\
*"\uc0\u920 \u949 \u8056 \u962  \u7952 \u966 \u945 \u957 \u949 \u961 \u974 \u952 \u951  \u7952 \u957  \u963 \u945 \u961 \u954 \u943 ..."*\
*"God was manifest in the flesh..."*\
\
### **The earlier Greek manuscripts (Sinaiticus, Alexandrinus, etc.) read:**\
*"\uc0\u8003 \u962  \u7952 \u966 \u945 \u957 \u949 \u961 \u974 \u952 \u951  \u7952 \u957  \u963 \u945 \u961 \u954 \u943 ..."*\
*"He/who was manifest in the flesh..."*\
\
**The difference is a single letter in Greek:**\
- **\uc0\u920 \u931 ** (contracted form of \u920 \u949 \u972 \u962  \'97 "God")\
- **\uc0\u927 \u931 ** (the relative pronoun "who")\
\
---\
\
## **Careful Textual Assessment**\
\
Under our maxim regarding the LXX for the OT, we noted the Geneva follows a text tradition that at times differs from the earlier LXX manuscripts. For the NT, similar textual questions arise.\
\
**The earlier Greek manuscripts consistently read "\uc0\u8005 \u962 " (who) rather than "\u920 \u949 \u972 \u962 " (God).**\
\
**Many careful scholars (including many who hold high views of Christ) acknowledge that the original reading was likely "\uc0\u8005 \u962 "** \'97 *"who was manifest in the flesh."* The change to "\u920 \u949 \u972 \u962 " appears to have happened later in the manuscript tradition, possibly through a scribal error (a small horizontal line added inside the omicron) that was then propagated.\
\
**Under our maxim #1 (let God be true):** We should hold to what the earliest and best manuscripts reveal about the original text.\
\
---\
\
## **Under Both Readings**\
\
### **Reading with "\uc0\u8005 \u962 " (who):**\
\
*"Great is the mystery of godliness: **who** was manifest in the flesh, justified in the Spirit, seen of angels, preached unto the Gentiles, believed on in the world, received up into glory."*\
\
Under this reading, the antecedent of "who" is the **mystery** \'97 meaning the Person of Christ is the subject of what follows. Christ was manifest in flesh (the incarnation). No claim is made that "God" (the Father) was manifest in flesh in a way that would collapse Persons.\
\
### **Reading with "\uc0\u920 \u949 \u972 \u962 " (God):**\
\
*"Great is the mystery of godliness: **God** was manifest in the flesh..."*\
\
Under this reading, "God" refers to a divine reality manifested in Christ. But note: even here, under your Name-authority framework, this reads as **"the Father's fullness was manifested bodily in the Man Christ Jesus"** (Col 2:9) \'97 not that the Father Himself became flesh, but that the divine nature the Son bore was manifested in His incarnation.\
\
**Under our maxims, either reading is compatible with our framework** \'97 but the textual evidence favors "\uc0\u8005 \u962 " (who), which does not press any metaphysical claim that we must labor to reconcile.\
\
